#{[17592186054365 :question/problem "Consider the following statement:\n\n$14$ is divisible by $4$ **or** $\\sqrt{3}$ is a rational number.\n\nWrite this statement in symbolic form using the following notations:\n\n- ![](images/64/64c5006a1d5f6b515ebfb603e5da3d10b44616960afd0dbbcabb32a5a23aed26.svg)\n\n- ![](images/b2/b2d623c77373a42771d812e0242d4545884a79107c1e97769d0f4bb8bb7a7a06.svg)"] [17592186054366 :question/problem "$7$ is odd **or** a multiple of $3$, **and** $3$ is less than $7$.\n\nExpress the compound statement above in symbolic form given the following notations:\n\n- ![](images/c9/c99c7535c3d2d6c854aea2e3408d15f2c0ff6423c4010ad211dbb6d576fe6928.svg)\n\n- ![](images/3f/3fca3aace2f71ead6ca860e93e9138abb40559e0eb9434ca068b563380b12912.svg)\n\n- ![](images/76/768f38af5ffc9fc48817db2f054dc9da251a26469a644c0e7eb7141fef54284b.svg)"] [17592186054495 :question/worked-solution "First, we decompose all of the terms into their prime factors.\n\n![](images/51/51bf9631f8b2ca90bda343a84a675f302ebae205446e528f5f05a8ea1f5f577b.svg)\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $y$'s.\n\n![](images/f3/f3edb95bc0a0a57a6dc439a06e1653d95c23dcd49e701b4d4d3f3d8c0f36c594.svg)\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\dfrac{3}{y+1}.$"] [17592186054496 :question/worked-solution "First, we decompose all of the terms into their prime factors. The $20$ in the numerator  becomes $2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 5,$ and the $8(z-1)$ in the denominator becomes $2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot (z-1).$\n\n![](images/47/47eb2277b9892fbd83cb1ae58c1c2a7640c29326402970254fb119c8c6c9dd5f.svg)\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there are two pairs of $2$'s.\n\n![](images/28/28f5bd692129118ef790ef1dde224e18f0a70674bf6ccd19eed4dbdb78b7b1f6.svg)\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\dfrac{5}{2(z-1)}.$"] [17592186054503 :question/problem "Given ![](images/07/07c13e831a55b71f20d0528b176ff156792a056c7c9188de233a2bdfe42e7e60.png) find ![](images/e8/e8cb7d81cd1994fca0e49f8489930e7be7efba436e4dabd12987a034733d7220.png)"] [17592186054503 :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to ![](images/f3/f321eb1d3d227339cd1ea5506e2aca012c961900c1a4ca6378e2371ccdd6695c.png) assuming that ![](images/7e/7e00fb1efa8367f76f7d901aebcae62996084c2c98e489f9b4ad87a9c6f788b6.png)\n\n![](images/8b/8b0ae15727b08a1ef06a810fb5c5bfb550a1064195632714013a79314cdbddeb.png)\n\nNow we solve the equation for ![](images/bf/bf12c4267d83a00c07e465de18ca07f56f0405db5214119d523c3d48aea663b9.png) as follows:\n\n![](images/f2/f2f87a7dd7745cf10ddc7133eb70db335e1d664c7f0a3f4b88becc8e0a5461f6.png)"] [17592186054582 :question/worked-solution "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n![](images/af/af9c822ae7190d9d82dbdd5024b5393a912b0cce5af42f39bcb5d37c3e675283.svg)\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n![](images/11/111f8df85bc2af18c21b63b254f6d3b679cb9b3a6a1ce35e9b0e4ef85f96ae07.svg)\nWe can now write the Taylor series for $f'(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty (x-3)^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nComparing the series above with the given series, we can deduce that\n\n$$\na_n = (x-3)^{n-1}.\n$$"] [17592186054590 :question/worked-solution "First, we decompose all of the terms into their prime factors. The $6y(x-2)$ in the numerator  becomes $2\\cdot 3 \\cdot y \\cdot (x-2),$ and the $9yz$ in the denominator becomes $3\\cdot 3 \\cdot y \\cdot z.$\n\n![](images/70/70068644b054adae3915b30b4c8fe3745190ec2a9eef2b1672528189e8dab9fd.svg)\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $3$'s and one pair of $y$'s.\n\n![](images/38/382b839130e9e8b59572303be4644e8c45c2be55c4b9f45ff2bd664037eecf8e.svg)\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\dfrac{2(x - 2)}{3z}.$"] [17592186054591 :question/worked-solution "First, we decompose all of the terms into their prime factors. The $6a(c+1)$ in the numerator becomes $2\\cdot 3 \\cdot a \\cdot (c+1),$ and the $3b(c+1)$ in the denominator becomes $3\\cdot b \\cdot (c+1).$\n\n![](images/7d/7d19b8a64cafa7f78bdf3795eba734282ad0a158b26ce12978f474aa12c84211.svg)\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $3$'s and one pair of $(c+1)$'s.\n\n![](images/72/72b3b315f67d06181cc1457b16fcee0e886c224960030a5975723761513e2371.svg)\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\dfrac{2a}{b}.$"] [17592186054592 :question/problem "Find the inverse of the following matrix: \n\n![](images/42/42d90774a0662b1c07746f930f9b7ae1dd5fb47723bdd82ad07f0a9cbeb20229.svg)"] [17592186054592 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given matrix is diagonal.\n\nThe inverse of a diagonal matrix can be found as follows: \n\n![](images/3a/3a76a7e21c5d897dcb97c3db89a81f9890c42a731614a700b00e6dc5dc2420e8.svg)\n\nIn our case, we obtain\n\n![](images/10/101fa4a623f3a31339469fa4ca2d5b074b1500fbe2c90eae3af5249380266519.svg)"] [17592186054593 :question/problem "Find the inverse of the following matrix:\n\n![](images/6c/6c0f32f6d17b2c34a1c574f737a518b61fc90ce7266b829ed034f65778319fb2.svg)"] [17592186054594 :question/problem "Find the inverse of the following matrix:\n\n![](images/66/66e85832b6e97aaf425f7cfe62c900cf0d476a4d3418405d6b029d7c04dbbff8.svg)"] [17592186055927 :tutorial/content "We're now able to prove the following result:\n\n![](images/43/439752d1d1d2d58f72839646de282cb987a26fe34e64831609b41a82dc1527cb.svg)\n\nFirst, we set \n\n![](images/f3/f3ef524df5ab32cde6c5682bdd188ad71e2d7c5e17e33924c4922690c9a1e3d1.svg) \nOur goal is to find ![](images/c7/c7244a31f756199a29c0e7e755eccdf9274346e643e0856566469b35a207e53a.svg)\n\nFirst, note that since ![](images/79/7985253d838299238e3faf6ec581b3b9dbe915a85722df9603c3aa11fc6e2990.svg) we have\n\n![](images/4e/4e736e470bb01bf341195a833a172b7284cd6d5284f2256fa149be409f043901.svg)\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to ![](images/5a/5aac2c81021f3f9e468e7840c5f95ece35c10b894bdae19ec1d247bdeb47ac6f.svg) we get the following:\n\n![](images/9e/9eae12637052db2e76d66bb39a24492924a69827b818cae0d47b8a881adea1d6.svg)\n\nFinally, since ![](images/c7/c7ac0127049c8701655a3f89e3698cee38c3fa26e3cdbc7161c1de3dc11a862c.svg) we conclude\n\n![](images/2f/2fba7b56204f6f63d0b1edcb20567c44c17144f94e4efb4803ab9a1f3e43805f.svg)\nas required."] [17592186055928 :tutorial/content "| Reading Time (minutes) | Frequency |\n| --- | --- |\n| ![](images/eb/eb2d393a97161602fda0e09b287e26997ebae14155e87da157a5b264a49b85fd.svg) | ![](images/0c/0cf2eee0cdec31b7acdc244a255aaee7d6d520cfeee67b64ac9cf215f5377477.svg) |\n| ![](images/57/576a22e89e78d499d45f4672ba1692d96519dc40bff4a5e86bb479c43560f15e.svg) | ![](images/e2/e2574f61b59164e433af2aeeb4c0dd160bf84436387873b2af855fa2770b5753.svg) |\n| ![](images/b9/b9a13aa77fd23827189ecc93136c4ea11e996dc88c781ded1c482d9435465f90.svg) | ![](images/c7/c700188055bde6c02303571bfb6b737fddda4a14c08edf1cdeeaf684530eea88.svg) |\n| ![](images/a9/a9657573fbe8954bb442bc38c87aa5c738b35bb2ce9637129083e874e6ba7b4b.svg) | ![](images/a1/a1a773b3227320e041bd84b2c7416eed5014bbb3741f6cf4e29846299a60c8fe.svg) |\n\nWe wish to find ![](images/80/80274aae659257c0607b95e5916b08cf0be5558d44ea2037cdd0c90d89583929.svg) the mean time library members spend in the reading room.\n\nSince we don't know exactly how long each member spent in the reading room, finding a precise answer for the mean is impossible. Therefore, the best we can do is *estimate* the mean.\n\nTo estimate the mean, we assume that the reading times within each class interval are uniformly distributed. Under this assumption, the expected reading time within each class is equal to the *midpoint* of that class. We therefore use each class midpoint to estimate the mean reading time of members in that class.\n\nTo find the midpoint of an interval ![](images/e7/e7b0d4e58a3354314e9548123fef2b6f26bf4b904906ce46f4ff23052ee71b79.svg) we take the mean of the endpoints:\n![](images/09/093db071be1338a842e84e139be5e929a05a8e59f987297b0409658907921fcc.svg)\n\nFor example, we can find the midpoint of the first class ![](images/30/303f2e5aa1407920ca6df2845186efbc1ef3942d976bdd897e0505f55b877ab7.svg) which we'll denote as ![](images/eb/ebe4b5414505776cd51e2aedf6771ef9812ada7e059a597eb8c5bcb3c117839b.svg) as follows:\n\n![](images/f0/f021a32a7e9f880ade9511018991f9b357a963687b61d8f903ecdddbd0f1c6e7.svg)\n\nContinuing this for the second, third, and fourth classes, we get the following table:\n\n| Reading Time (minutes) | ![](images/53/534dc8cbe8df49870c1b013c78b51db358835105931d1dd11bd7af060af5397b.svg) | ![](images/92/929c2a5e3b92ad47814e45bba04dc753a740827c64678968b9cfe25dedf794fa.svg) |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ![](images/eb/eb2d393a97161602fda0e09b287e26997ebae14155e87da157a5b264a49b85fd.svg) | ![](images/0c/0cf2eee0cdec31b7acdc244a255aaee7d6d520cfeee67b64ac9cf215f5377477.svg) | ![](images/e2/e2574f61b59164e433af2aeeb4c0dd160bf84436387873b2af855fa2770b5753.svg) |\n| ![](images/57/576a22e89e78d499d45f4672ba1692d96519dc40bff4a5e86bb479c43560f15e.svg) | ![](images/e2/e2574f61b59164e433af2aeeb4c0dd160bf84436387873b2af855fa2770b5753.svg) | ![](images/a8/a87f83b52273ea3023379ebe633c0190b90efeee390efda869402c7dde411e73.svg) |\n| ![](images/b9/b9a13aa77fd23827189ecc93136c4ea11e996dc88c781ded1c482d9435465f90.svg) | ![](images/c7/c700188055bde6c02303571bfb6b737fddda4a14c08edf1cdeeaf684530eea88.svg) | ![](images/bc/bc98a2fdf686f851fd343e6cca8ebf25186c6d4fe401df573939f8e5e1d5d044.svg) |\n| ![](images/a9/a9657573fbe8954bb442bc38c87aa5c738b35bb2ce9637129083e874e6ba7b4b.svg) | ![](images/a1/a1a773b3227320e041bd84b2c7416eed5014bbb3741f6cf4e29846299a60c8fe.svg) | ![](images/75/75729a0d755ed343ab24a2288c1c0edc765a2cffd3a70e982daf9b2b68785969.svg) |\n\nFor notational convenience, we've denoted ![](images/53/534dc8cbe8df49870c1b013c78b51db358835105931d1dd11bd7af060af5397b.svg) as the frequency associated with the ![](images/33/33f73a318016d781b6ae1aa913c2f1807ab5c7e6c2901e508fc56561fbe26b45.svg)th class.\n\nNext, we wish to estimate the total time members in each class spent in the reading room. To do this, we multiply the midpoint of each class with the respective frequency:\n\n| Reading Time (minutes) | ![](images/53/534dc8cbe8df49870c1b013c78b51db358835105931d1dd11bd7af060af5397b.svg) | ![](images/92/929c2a5e3b92ad47814e45bba04dc753a740827c64678968b9cfe25dedf794fa.svg) | ![](images/5e/5e0b9d9429241d06dd5542774b9c4bd54f4571ff89bd74e160f3a7521f8c190f.svg) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ![](images/eb/eb2d393a97161602fda0e09b287e26997ebae14155e87da157a5b264a49b85fd.svg) | ![](images/0c/0cf2eee0cdec31b7acdc244a255aaee7d6d520cfeee67b64ac9cf215f5377477.svg) | ![](images/e2/e2574f61b59164e433af2aeeb4c0dd160bf84436387873b2af855fa2770b5753.svg) | ![](images/bc/bc98a2fdf686f851fd343e6cca8ebf25186c6d4fe401df573939f8e5e1d5d044.svg) |\n| ![](images/57/576a22e89e78d499d45f4672ba1692d96519dc40bff4a5e86bb479c43560f15e.svg) | ![](images/e2/e2574f61b59164e433af2aeeb4c0dd160bf84436387873b2af855fa2770b5753.svg) | ![](images/a8/a87f83b52273ea3023379ebe633c0190b90efeee390efda869402c7dde411e73.svg) | ![](images/b7/b7abb3e7de01b392b61c0d52adbb029f892b572ccf561ae4cbb184559662b433.svg) |\n| ![](images/b9/b9a13aa77fd23827189ecc93136c4ea11e996dc88c781ded1c482d9435465f90.svg) | ![](images/c7/c700188055bde6c02303571bfb6b737fddda4a14c08edf1cdeeaf684530eea88.svg) | ![](images/bc/bc98a2fdf686f851fd343e6cca8ebf25186c6d4fe401df573939f8e5e1d5d044.svg) | ![](images/fa/faccc7daaa6b9ec86f1b555e17dacd85579cfce2efcbe51da99715b81f955c31.svg) |\n| ![](images/a9/a9657573fbe8954bb442bc38c87aa5c738b35bb2ce9637129083e874e6ba7b4b.svg) | ![](images/a1/a1a773b3227320e041bd84b2c7416eed5014bbb3741f6cf4e29846299a60c8fe.svg) | ![](images/75/75729a0d755ed343ab24a2288c1c0edc765a2cffd3a70e982daf9b2b68785969.svg) | ![](images/57/570557e20085be9afbb51a5576b45fd586673fb0427851b8290e82da02d3bdc1.svg) |\n\nFinally, to estimate the mean ![](images/80/80274aae659257c0607b95e5916b08cf0be5558d44ea2037cdd0c90d89583929.svg) we sum the values in the last column (our estimate of the total time members spent in the reading room) and divide by ![](images/a8/a87f83b52273ea3023379ebe633c0190b90efeee390efda869402c7dde411e73.svg) (the total number of observations).\n\n![](images/38/38fe74fd7100dcd1827bc3392d48d24146b1558fe98f0c2ca6a1a09ccb771044.svg)\nrounded to one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, the mean time spent in the reading room is approximately ![](images/fb/fbf917e631efb5fbe1edda4c931a66ca12357a2e229ff8875380a7fa8b4222fd.svg) minutes."] [17592186055929 :tutorial/content "If we have a data set that consists of the points ![](images/97/970506054c9e79c03a528201181319553f832d0a5ecaa39d86dc3d32a24aac2d.svg) then the variance, denoted by ![](images/15/15773375325018481cf6af2b18349d4af08f0e4675f16b35018e322e65b7978c.svg) is defined as\n\n![](images/43/4383e314b0902a782293099b329c4fe6ca0fe27122be0e7dc39a79ab5db86ee7.svg)\n\nwhere ![](images/ff/ff1f8ee2c66cdaacecf61d002cfb371021034fd76abd86acfb5636cb5af7d765.svg) is the mean of the data set. \n\nGiven a frequency table containing continuous data split into ![](images/24/24733a44cf365eb5e1b9257432a747a5a38e67a72d9b626e1b88f921cb9be2d7.svg) classes, we can estimate the variance by treating all observations within each class as though they were equal to the midpoint of that class. This gives\n![](images/ec/ec206e4f1a0ca4c7cf7a6588a6dc414820cb9ac1a46e4f659152c9b2fdd7f0c8.svg)\nwhere\n\n- ![](images/53/534dc8cbe8df49870c1b013c78b51db358835105931d1dd11bd7af060af5397b.svg) is the frequency of the ![](images/33/33f73a318016d781b6ae1aa913c2f1807ab5c7e6c2901e508fc56561fbe26b45.svg)th class,\n\n- ![](images/92/929c2a5e3b92ad47814e45bba04dc753a740827c64678968b9cfe25dedf794fa.svg) is the midpoint of the ![](images/33/33f73a318016d781b6ae1aa913c2f1807ab5c7e6c2901e508fc56561fbe26b45.svg)th class,\n\n- ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) is the total number of observations, and\n\n- ![](images/ff/ff1f8ee2c66cdaacecf61d002cfb371021034fd76abd86acfb5636cb5af7d765.svg) is the estimated mean, calculated using the class midpoints.\n\nWhen using a sample to estimate the variance of a larger population, we often divide by ![](images/75/754b6ff2738e2326f30da550a535ffba774e8bbc4c3f9289d93e9a50f97c9998.svg) instead of ![](images/c2/c2475d72e6ec36042292de6c0160930174172b2386148d823a70e59fddfb93c2.svg) We use the notation ![](images/35/35b71bd6f446cec40dee1ad04f03e2406091f6285528b9966fc8ec9b2f4dcc04.svg) here to emphasize that we're dividing by ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg).\n\nAlso, note that if ![](images/ff/ff1f8ee2c66cdaacecf61d002cfb371021034fd76abd86acfb5636cb5af7d765.svg) is unknown, it must be estimated using the previously described method. \n\nLet's see an example."] [17592186055936 :tutorial/content "The **greatest common factor** (or ![](images/86/861891fd828ee3558330774b23f5dcee69976d8f302da6f1e6825bf3b4be47e6.svg)) of ![](images/f5/f5a17e5e4e88d2fd8eed0ac09f0e02d77507121fac42d5f696cf407aaab6185c.svg) and ![](images/2f/2fd89ffbc3140e9c2f12576c1dec2522d603c45e8b1d4128c02892f651c6e6bb.svg) is the largest expression that will divide *both* ![](images/f5/f5a17e5e4e88d2fd8eed0ac09f0e02d77507121fac42d5f696cf407aaab6185c.svg) and ![](images/2f/2fd89ffbc3140e9c2f12576c1dec2522d603c45e8b1d4128c02892f651c6e6bb.svg) with no remainder.\n\nTo find the GCF, we list the factors of the coefficients and then identify any variable factors the expressions have in common.\n\nThe factors of ![](images/f5/f5a17e5e4e88d2fd8eed0ac09f0e02d77507121fac42d5f696cf407aaab6185c.svg) are\n![](images/05/05dbff97b169f70d1dd03beb2c23670b48520c5d98a839d218db50a06518fbf6.svg)\n\nThe factors of ![](images/34/34d666f013d8ce4f2b50c0a9ab9c2b5808feb37163ba38470555867babada4ef.svg) are\n![](images/59/59919e2659e3bb232e4dbc1857c9f0eca27706bbad72aea6a55bb749706307a2.svg)\n\nThe common factors of ![](images/f5/f5a17e5e4e88d2fd8eed0ac09f0e02d77507121fac42d5f696cf407aaab6185c.svg) and ![](images/34/34d666f013d8ce4f2b50c0a9ab9c2b5808feb37163ba38470555867babada4ef.svg) are\n![](images/b0/b08502484874a42582d63ddfc0eda0048576d0cc460aced250ad962721d0e985.svg)\n\nThe greatest of these common factors is ![](images/fe/fe34f715005da1a8b682c9650a76785673fdf25c3b7c7546bb94824470c54caf.svg)\n\nNow we look at the variable factors. Since ![](images/f5/f5a17e5e4e88d2fd8eed0ac09f0e02d77507121fac42d5f696cf407aaab6185c.svg) has no variable and ![](images/2f/2fd89ffbc3140e9c2f12576c1dec2522d603c45e8b1d4128c02892f651c6e6bb.svg) contains ![](images/5a/5aac2c81021f3f9e468e7840c5f95ece35c10b894bdae19ec1d247bdeb47ac6f.svg) there are no variable factors in common.\n\nThus, the greatest common factor of ![](images/f5/f5a17e5e4e88d2fd8eed0ac09f0e02d77507121fac42d5f696cf407aaab6185c.svg) and ![](images/2f/2fd89ffbc3140e9c2f12576c1dec2522d603c45e8b1d4128c02892f651c6e6bb.svg) is ![](images/fe/fe34f715005da1a8b682c9650a76785673fdf25c3b7c7546bb94824470c54caf.svg)"] [17592186055952 :tutorial/content "Consider the graph of the parabola ![](images/f2/f218c572691a74963cd3808bf4df2923122e84b65d1fabf1138bab6264b043b5.svg) shown below.\n\n![](images/45/450251899f728a3cc927b72fdb7655204828c8e0be0432cb08cee2462d6e5bfe.png)\n\nWe can describe many features of the graph using mathematical terminology:\n\n- The ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-intercepts of the parabola are at ![](images/81/8171bb8403bd612f56ef5f32f400f1aef8efeed51ea279d36779771fdea1ae0d.svg) and ![](images/77/779cec712506e0f798805963ab85841b46650fb22681d97a64aec4b85a47d07f.svg) We say that ![](images/b0/b064281df4bef2506521322c7477468b9cc565616e2c5b6f21000df6522399c1.svg) and ![](images/1f/1fb9f2f73a09599931892c1dee7629975b4d851e5dd6f0d07b7c641bbf17bf54.svg) are the **roots** (or **zeros**) of the parabola.\n\n- The parabola intersects the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis at  ![](images/6a/6a0e2cbf9f56c3c989ad96f020fe8bb0f7c785ff6de8073c476232b95476ac13.svg). We say that ![](images/78/78d4465d85b55cee3832a9b598a2564b6a3fa9a072de8a1fb4b40ca884079670.svg) is the **![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept**. The ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept is the same as the constant term of the function.\n\n- The parabola has a \"turning point\" at  ![](images/ef/ef43561f9d825c9e79fb03dffafa744d386d0c2a5a07da903675bca120d59fd1.svg) which is called the **vertex**. In an upwards parabola (like the one shown above), the vertex is the minimum point, while in a downwards parabola, the vertex is the maximum point.\n\n- The vertical line  ![](images/df/df9822243c11ec9f0fabca5cc578976d0bdea2c521b4f61964f5f3850a970504.svg) that passes through the vertex is called the **axis of symmetry**. The part of the parabola to the left of the axis of symmetry is the mirror image of the right, and vice versa.\n\n- This particular parabola is **upward opening** because it has a distinctive ![](images/13/13ab0b72e7963f3e74404b8b75f12654ece94c2c44e1fb09fdf230b976eb681b.svg) shape. A parabola ![](images/7c/7c49eede8f0eb80dadd8384ae942dc8248ef454eeb6afa1aeba7ae48e7545456.svg) is upward opening if the quadratic coefficient is positive ![](images/d2/d2a0fc4d90b45e06999c89b1a40ff6f01116e9c429c195adbfced573f9e47660.svg)\n\n- Parabolas can also be **downward opening** with a distinctive ![](images/57/57872695daf3067460adec5a506cfb1457729de4eeac879eab9f7355570bac11.svg) shape. A parabola ![](images/7c/7c49eede8f0eb80dadd8384ae942dc8248ef454eeb6afa1aeba7ae48e7545456.svg) is downward opening if the quadratic coefficient is negative ![](images/a1/a16d199f292dbd74941402b3d485cf107a7a985d28aeb13ab50ff6baf87f2d4b.svg)\n\nLet's take a look at an example of a downward opening parabola."] [17592186055982 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to solve the following system of equations:\n![](images/01/01eee66dd39797b90503716f097ff604a4f339d8ea0a25ceef9b8ea7a0898ab4.svg)\n\nTo solve systems of equations *graphically*, we first draw each of the equations in the same coordinate system. Then, the solution of the system is given by the common points where the lines intersect.\n\nLet's see how this method works for the system above. To plot the functions given by the equations, we first rewrite them by making ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg) the subject in each equation.\n![](images/c7/c7778dd2c82032ef68ad8221a990abe1b2bafb4e36a7a0e2640a43856be58c5a.svg) \n\nThen, we draw the lines in the same coordinate system:\n\n![](images/47/47fb02cf8bb4668b24034302fd4265ec5261f4d244cd9d9d599afeffd91d2424.png)\n\nThe lines intersect at the point ![](images/d8/d87c68d6226f4bb9ecf52ab576a5ac45c83fbb02c83edd1af04ac235dd518467.svg), which is the solution to our system.\n\n![](images/b3/b3b95135ad9a758f891280b15e8bd6f6165bf9f47a76a1c99280af02dad78911.png)\n\nWe can also use the graphical method to solve systems of equations where the corresponding functions are not straight lines. These types of systems are called **nonlinear systems**. Let's consider such an example."] [17592186055988 :tutorial/content "If we are given one or more factors of a polynomial, we can use these factors to continue factoring the polynomial completely. We just need to divide the polynomial by the known factors, and then continue factoring the quotient.\n\nFor example, suppose we know that $(x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x) = x^4+4x^3+x^2-6x.$ Then $(x+2)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n![](images/32/328addabb6a0f282635b7647ca26cedfeeaf6c6fbc105c0243cb1abe29f8608f.svg)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^4+4x^3+x^2-6x}{x+2} = x^3+2x^2-3x,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^4+4x^3+x^2-6x = (x+2) (x^3+2x^2-3x).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the cubic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)({x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-3x) & =(x+2)(x)({x}^{2}+2x-3) \\\\  & =(x+2)(x)(x+3)(x-1) \\\\  & =x(x+2)(x+3)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186055989 :tutorial/content "Functions are usually given in the form ![](images/44/44c838deb3fa8e3182fbd9d4a03f964dc2731730559d00328190e9259bf01762.svg) where ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg) is explicitly written as a function of ![](images/cf/cff7b257e3c71ec70dc7cd273bdd1cced4254993a43cffbfc338460c21bc7319.svg) However, they can also be written in **implicit** form, like\n![](images/82/82728d8a7de8d66f1952d6404473ba2e8130c13c9536b2d335b05684c9e9b382.svg)\n\nWhen a function is given in implicit form, we can differentiate it using the method of **implicit differentiation**, which consists of the following two steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to ![](images/cf/cff7b257e3c71ec70dc7cd273bdd1cced4254993a43cffbfc338460c21bc7319.svg)\n\nWe need to remember that ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg) *is a function of* ![](images/5a/5aac2c81021f3f9e468e7840c5f95ece35c10b894bdae19ec1d247bdeb47ac6f.svg) i.e., ![](images/6f/6f3c9a64baef025798baeebc2d92c4361ec210293ebf0b0b5c64055541a50ae3.svg) So, we apply the chain rule:\n![](images/7d/7d48f6e8d69bab060f3e0a41927e97481d0a8f05b424b8bdb5639dfcc58fbc76.svg)\n\n**Step 2**: Solve the equation for ![](images/c7/c7244a31f756199a29c0e7e755eccdf9274346e643e0856566469b35a207e53a.svg)\n![](images/80/80fd42d93eb7ab2cce44347268e404853f4cf507896d8097af70a92323962377.svg)\n\nSo, the derivative of ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg) is ![](images/42/420cc2cbdc995a548ee077e5547ca1522bd2c1cdbbd31f304a4466497acacb5c.svg)"] [17592186055992 :tutorial/content "For some types of matrices, finding the inverse is relatively easy. One of the more common examples of this is diagonal matrices.\n\nA diagonal matrix is invertible only if it has nonzero entries on its main diagonal. In such cases, we have \n\n![](images/a4/a4a9e91984e03a3e3eed46b219de35d70d12eaa55afde8c189ceedd62cb2cca5.svg)\n\nFor example, \n\n![](images/93/93723194b30c2be2150f5f91d9bcb7955fe46e4b76fd439669ad93483e141f92.svg)"] [17592186055994 :tutorial/content "An **atomic triangular matrix** or **Frobenius matrix** is a square matrix with the following properties:\n\n- all entries on the main diagonal are ones\n\n- the matrix has only one column where some entries below the main diagonal can be non-zero\n\n- all other entries outside the main diagonal equal zero\n\nThe following matrix is an example of a Frobenius matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNote that this matrix has nonzero entries only in the first column and on the main diagonal, and all of the main diagonal entries equal one.\n\nWe have the following theorem regarding Frobenius matrices:\n\n*If $F$ is a Frobenius matrix, then its inverse is also a Frobenius matrix, where the corresponding nonzero entries outside the main diagonal have opposite signs.*\n\nUsing this theorem, we get \n![](images/69/6909755578de3e528497a2736a6a6d789255cb81b39eb4ecfcd0747178813056.svg)"] [17592186056042 :tutorial/content "An **ordered pair** $(x, y)$ is a pair of mathematical objects $x$ and $y$ written in parentheses and separated by a comma. \n\nYou are already familiar with an ordered pair $(x,y)$ representing a point in the Cartesian plane. In this context, $x$ and $y$ are real numbers. However, more generally, the coordinates $x$ and $y$ of an ordered pair can be any mathematical objects we like! Moreover, they do not need to be the same type of object. \n\nHere are some examples of ordered pairs.\n\n![](images/bf/bf99b45f9e9ab363314f3604ef6316c7fe7e63623452f2fe78d597dd60c2d302.svg)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We say that an ordered pair $(x,y)$ has **coordinates** $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Given two ordered pairs $(a, b)$ and $(c, d),$ we say they're **equal** if $a = c$ and $b = d,$ and we write \n\n$$\n(a, b) = (c, d).\n$$\n\nFor example, $(2, 3) = (2, 3)$ but $(2, 3) \\ne (3, 2).$\n\nWe use ordered pairs to define the so-called **Cartesian product** of two sets. Let's discuss this in more detail."] [17592186059452 :question/problem "Find the value of ![](images/8e/8e7935fce161e2a1ed706ec03bb5cd202e8c4650ba01ca5e4633f9f1c121d04a.png)"] [17592186059453 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $A$ given below?\n\n- $\\det(A) = 0$\n\n- $A$ is **not** a singular matrix\n\n- $A$ is a singular matrix\n\n![](images/21/21cd71e00c682ad1117b9ae126adcbeb8e4c55769c73eae1e1169534f2a01d4a.png)"] [17592186059458 :question/problem "For the function $g(x)$ defined on the set of integers, find $g(-12)$ and $g(3),$ where $g(x)$ is the following piecewise function:\n![](images/43/43ec07dcf63fdaf3f85bd576c64da4f4afccee23143cfe51c337dd3e3e855cce.svg)"] [17592186059459 :question/problem "Given that\n![](images/cf/cfe7164dbaeba6215a7fcb84fd9bf192a19c6165562ef124344258df3fcafde6.svg)\nwhat is the graph of $y=f(x)?$"] [17592186059519 :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac{c}{d}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac ba =\\dfrac dc\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 23 = \\dfrac 2x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 32 = \\dfrac x2\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the multiplication principle:\n\n![](images/8e/8e7b2d027a761ba93f679cfb517709811938da412b06e59212391741b5660a2b.svg)"] [17592186059520 :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=-1$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac{c}{d}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac ba =\\dfrac dc\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1{x+1} = 2 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac {x+1}{1} = \\dfrac 12\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\nx+1 = \\dfrac 12 .\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the addition principle:\n\n![](images/d7/d7150c6093121f9f6dc0ecd9675d5be8f0855d339f3404d7a357875362d9990f.svg)"] [17592186059560 :question/problem "Calculate the minor of the entry $b_{21}$ in the matrix\n![](images/8e/8e664813c6467d5bb06a8eb1e1d68778d3142479939624a7a46674f593d786b9.png)"] [17592186059560 :question/worked-solution "The minor of $b_{21} = 3$ (second row, first column) is the determinant of the remaining entries when the column and row containing $3$ are removed: \n\n![](images/a6/a69955fcba387ef3c65f1c1529c2007d8d70f82d95738af9b995999a21198730.png)\n\nSo, the minor of $b_{21}=3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2-5\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =2-35 \\\\  & =-33.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186059561 :question/problem "What is the minor of $c_{22}$ in the matrix\n![](images/14/1449dfd139653f75de7be2e27e86f0c9d2d8a5a17e25ae184b892f489ffbb71e.png)"] [17592186059561 :question/worked-solution "First, we remove the second row and second column: \n\n![](images/09/09bd73c7e51abcad8efa354317519a2687601b3ccdbfea0ac2b6222f75b3397e.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det\\begin{bmatrix}7x & -3y \\\\ -y & 5\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}7x & -3y \\\\ -y & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =7x\\cdot 5-(-3y)\\cdot (-y) \\\\  & =35x-3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186059562 :question/problem "Given that the minor of $a_{23}$ is $5,$ find all the possible values of $k$ in the matrix\n![](images/1d/1d37ba9039f24a96be2d148c9f30c8a10ca30c9745ad8a28df23de5a42f6dcab.png)"] [17592186059562 :question/worked-solution "First, we remove the second row and third column:\n\n![](images/80/80f9b95139f7b571c7752ee1feec4c9d3df8456223581736ae019344a6f9a080.png)\n\nThe minor of $a_{23}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det\\begin{bmatrix}k & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2k\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}k & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =k\\cdot 2k-3\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =2{k}^{2}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the minor of $a_{23}$ is $5,$ so we must have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{k}^{2}+3 & =5 \\\\ 2{k}^{2} & =2 \\\\ {k}^{2} & =1 \\\\ k & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $k=-1$ and $k=1.$"] [17592186059563 :question/problem "Find the inverse of ![](images/89/8967a472cff03599b1c2c94704fc9dc7272dc2a344d1876309dce1010a7ecc1f.svg)"] [17592186059563 :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** Find the determinant of $A.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 8\\end{vmatrix} & =0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-(8-12)+2(-3-0) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -4 & -3 \\\\ 14 & -8 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n![](images/51/51ba8948d60b649bea88218e86b2ea39cbecfe8014367a3def09d2aeb7667460.svg)\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n![](images/b6/b6339df9152864283a9ed0b938c2183da94121edec8d5b434566b2953e5bd0b0.svg)\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-2}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -14 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -14 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{9}{2} & 7 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & -2 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186059564 :question/problem "The entry $a'_{31}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n![](images/4b/4b816f3bc511cd6170658d9b75715fc6385feeea1e768cd44d1bd199b67bf853.svg) \nequals $1$. Find the value of $k$."] [17592186059565 :question/problem "Determine the entry $a'_{23}$ in the inverse of the matrix ![](images/96/967f00655054db75990de13f3634f8d0b320ffde849057ce8cc9a19c58754aa2.svg)"] [17592186059608 :question/problem "If $f$ is the piecewise function\n![](images/e6/e6a8c403594625a24b7f5af7eb5909d626e0ffbba5fc9a4947b7d856013c2bfe.svg)\nwhat is the graph of $y=f(x)?$"] [17592186060870 :tutorial/content "A **piecewise function** is a function that is defined differently in different parts of the function's domain.\n\nFor example, consider the piecewise function ![](images/a8/a8e8a9c3f9a7bef1c9c40cad27eeb93267b8b30eb74ce36acd9bf90b461c21ae.svg) defined as follows:\n![](images/78/78daf51ed18581f874f275e211d9f6b651af383503fdbc6e04f7d06b083eaffd.svg)\nA plot of the function is given below:\n\n![](images/2a/2ad96db45424ed30eb3219a4ae36e13d5498a3719695ccb73a9313058f2fa15c.png)\n\nThe function has two distinct branches, one for  ![](images/a3/a3d6121723fd4e9feea862013f28e4326de8afabb08b46040ca1c80788500f84.svg) and one for  ![](images/ee/ee98d74fc058e95e72d13a5e575e70e4d5adedd26413e3f01ab1e3f788be87e8.svg). The function is a piecewise function because it has different definitions depending on where we are in the domain. \n\nSuppose that we want to calculate ![](images/0c/0c0dc4f7a3b289c14dd126e050458eab6b82ccccb923d9c6993ac5042303568e.svg) For this, we'd use the  left branch ![](images/a3/a3d6121723fd4e9feea862013f28e4326de8afabb08b46040ca1c80788500f84.svg) because ![](images/85/8521c0711d7afa3552209b26e08d80787124feea347c2cdfa81a8de5470ab58d.svg) On this branch, the function definition is ![](images/5d/5d344346082f57f1cd7456779e9a30d079068f78a466fe899ac376cfbd14dbaf.svg) so we have\n\n![](images/0e/0e990ed7688ceb39db8d63fd54c0f22f52f5260efb6636419319b92b43171610.svg)\n\nSimilarly, if we want to calculate ![](images/71/713ad3a0e19cfab825d702bd00e14c956db1844974c9da945d697930a02e7178.svg), we'd use the  right branch ![](images/ee/ee98d74fc058e95e72d13a5e575e70e4d5adedd26413e3f01ab1e3f788be87e8.svg) because ![](images/95/95a3686bf6cd3fddc29b98a39ab23027d1cc794ae2517aefde675a62f0a4d023.svg) On this branch, the function definition is ![](images/85/85f568b3e2d870a755a97fb3457f3c62e814f559b8b017ac05c291fb5d8aa79e.svg) so we have\n\n![](images/16/166c6a001bb66b83e3d369c2211f11baf60650e54c93da7bee47539fa717d44a.svg)"] [17592186060901 :tutorial/content "All the angles we have constructed so far have been inside the range ![](images/fa/fa8919b9a8968214967b7c4b868b1eb64a95c1d0d2068bd312a4a4bb11ad4e6f.svg) However, we can also construct angles outside this range.\n\nThe way we do this involves **coterminal angles**. Coterminal angles are different angles that appear as the same angle in the coordinate plane. For example, the angles ![](images/d2/d22511f2212b05e9408f52f8da19a125897737f033bb64f480780f2f024b2091.svg) and ![](images/6f/6f3e3ccd469bd4b7cf2baf7a17cad64bb11f3f6f2d202e79362b8c44df1fcdd2.svg) are coterminal because a rotation of ![](images/6f/6f3e3ccd469bd4b7cf2baf7a17cad64bb11f3f6f2d202e79362b8c44df1fcdd2.svg) is the same as the rotation of ![](images/d5/d5c42dddda6dd2e3707f543621c5ab3ef3557e26aa8e938ffddc686917238fdd.svg) In general, whenever we add or subtract a multiple of ![](images/43/431e60581c842f0bf0e2554ccfbe65001d79fd5493de301c6889f8237304b725.svg) we get a coterminal angle.\n\nThe standard way in which we draw a **positive** angle ![](images/06/06e47e2878930095abe73547809f3c9f87be3cd9b3b9069ca582970b60598840.svg) in the coordinate plane is as follows:\n\n- Subtract integer multiples of ![](images/6f/6f3e3ccd469bd4b7cf2baf7a17cad64bb11f3f6f2d202e79362b8c44df1fcdd2.svg) until we get a coterminal angle that lies in the range ![](images/29/29577be589a8a6614c131d995797fa1224c1df2f6b538a669e5fba718edb926b.svg)\n\n- Draw the resulting angle in the coordinate plane.\n\nFor example, to represent the angle ![](images/fa/fae5f1a011f55464bbc26a61e49aa6c4b2e2b9fb2076370a05f35c00de3a7a29.svg) in the coordinate plane, we first subtract ![](images/29/2903c04e0275e76d718eff7ae8277ecb8b5404777225212dca2507b93907ab41.svg)\n\n![](images/3b/3b3bf36f7e1ccf241036659c484ffa0dd17884f8044f6bd6faf6240764552c41.svg)\n\nThe result, ![](images/73/73c6bd81124f4f9c92e0d8ed37449d8dd5be227f9e4b05b2f7953d28ddff7d6c.svg) is a coterminal angle that lies in the range ![](images/29/29577be589a8a6614c131d995797fa1224c1df2f6b538a669e5fba718edb926b.svg) So, to draw the angle ![](images/63/637324a8ad2f6d52227a2fe46d938a816aab3de3d50e0f9ee035f602537e2091.svg) we draw the coterminal angle ![](images/33/33cc1d85e29c1ae4ce3b019b0e49a5dba77efefa6f405590a91fce42f504baf3.svg) in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/bb/bbeb3dfdcb56e4f7f1a39010dc798af004f3ab5ee843f653e86f80fe9974dd30.png)\n\n**Note:** For every multiple of ![](images/4b/4b6a99f552f4ddb574063b55a576d5ba9d4a8fbc2be8b87c8eb11dfee8331c34.svg) we subtract, we make one complete rotation counter-clockwise around the origin. A rotation of ![](images/4b/4b6a99f552f4ddb574063b55a576d5ba9d4a8fbc2be8b87c8eb11dfee8331c34.svg) is the same as the rotation of ![](images/57/5792ebfa34e0824d805857dcb2467c1c23d3786ae5a5bad16b0c1f6616b6b0bf.svg) so it doesn't contribute to our final angle.\n\nHere, we subtracted ![](images/4b/4b6a99f552f4ddb574063b55a576d5ba9d4a8fbc2be8b87c8eb11dfee8331c34.svg) a single time, so we make a single rotation.\n\n![](images/ac/ac16cf651b6f5e16d3f31418646388dac16384c4824e0095bf9a925c13b1ea77.png)"] [17592186060902 :tutorial/content "To find a coterminal angle of a given angle ![](images/1f/1f216b45120e801bbdcf2c3f165ac341a7dd0bd305f87381647b205831b06725.svg) we rotate ![](images/6f/6f3e3ccd469bd4b7cf2baf7a17cad64bb11f3f6f2d202e79362b8c44df1fcdd2.svg) clockwise or counter-clockwise from ![](images/b9/b98533dee87a7bd8ed4d593a2aabd517621d6ab96e40a5b2ab4a8ac1923ddac5.svg) We could do this again and again, each time looping by ![](images/6f/6f3e3ccd469bd4b7cf2baf7a17cad64bb11f3f6f2d202e79362b8c44df1fcdd2.svg) to find an infinite number of coterminal angles:\n![](images/bc/bcd7ec5620afdf6f142437b0a0aedcddcee4678309d9d2328da3892eb0034730.svg)\n\nFor instance, the angles ![](images/ed/ed05bb3b845471f7526114d38f3c1a525aeb653d50a50b3ebe0457266e275d5f.svg) ![](images/94/9476642d5d3f3e418c71f7b062f33f894fa648e3c0cee6a15fc359b43889c19a.svg) ![](images/94/94f4422d3745b4401e7c9517c9e08382bb4d4af59a4f9ef164500530b530a525.svg) and ![](images/98/98e07f13eb10e759924519f7b068aa9f9768dcd7c362088b89f1831c886a3a2d.svg) are all coterminal.\n\n  \n\n![](images/c2/c2949532fb08722c3dbaaad3cd971ae2c3d3e7d7606f051da6dccd2bc3cfecc6.png)"] [17592186060903 :tutorial/content "The method for constructing a *negative* angle ![](images/06/06e47e2878930095abe73547809f3c9f87be3cd9b3b9069ca582970b60598840.svg) outside the range ![](images/3b/3bbae843070f7afe0fc1c253cc8dd663ed4571cc5d250bebae728dd3298cee8a.svg) is similar to the method for positive angles, but with one main difference: we *add* multiples of ![](images/43/431e60581c842f0bf0e2554ccfbe65001d79fd5493de301c6889f8237304b725.svg) instead of subtracting. So the steps are:\n\n- Add integer multiples of ![](images/6f/6f3e3ccd469bd4b7cf2baf7a17cad64bb11f3f6f2d202e79362b8c44df1fcdd2.svg) until we get a coterminal angle that lies in the range ![](images/29/29577be589a8a6614c131d995797fa1224c1df2f6b538a669e5fba718edb926b.svg)\n\n- Draw the resulting angle in the coordinate plane.\n\nFor example, we can represent the angle ![](images/78/78826f360e99c31e2e800e0691e6770af9a57442c1b09212bb94f3df8a7a7e6c.svg) as follows:\n\n![](images/bc/bcc5c8dedaeca80ac8c48f4d5d18aa3666e87b5be942f1a49d187f99ade28435.svg)\n\nNow, we draw the angle ![](images/20/20615d4147fe63007acc775caaa7d175edca7e0717a9e1224b4a66d490df2316.svg) in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/78/785973780f0ed538c0a0f430a402c25fb562a7308911f1d5cecc958da02b99f2.png)\n\nAgain, for every multiple of ![](images/4b/4b6a99f552f4ddb574063b55a576d5ba9d4a8fbc2be8b87c8eb11dfee8331c34.svg) that we add, we make one complete rotation around the origin clockwise. A rotation of ![](images/4b/4b6a99f552f4ddb574063b55a576d5ba9d4a8fbc2be8b87c8eb11dfee8331c34.svg) is the same as the rotation of ![](images/57/5792ebfa34e0824d805857dcb2467c1c23d3786ae5a5bad16b0c1f6616b6b0bf.svg) so it doesn't contribute to our final angle."] [17592186060907 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given two vectors \n$\\mathbf{a} = \\langle 2,3 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b} = \\langle -4,4 \\rangle,$ shown below. How do we compute the vectors $\\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}$  and  $-5\\mathbf{a}$ in terms of the standard basis $\\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j}?$\n\n![](images/25/254809cbc834b493759a6fa09222a2c90c5fbfc3280d22e82470e760a7a4c508.png)\n\nTo compute $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b},$ we collect all of the $\\mathbf{i}$'s and $\\mathbf{j}$'s. We know that $\\mathbf{a} = {\\color{red}2}\\mathbf{i} + {\\color{blue}3}\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b} = {\\color{red}-4}\\mathbf{i} + {\\color{blue}4}\\mathbf{j}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =(2i+3j)+(-4i+4j) \\\\  & =(2+(-4))i+(3+4)j \\\\  & =-2i+7j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting our answer in angle bracket and column vector notations, we get \n![](images/ea/eae25a74dde73f7899f6ccbbdbb1a1f589fc72cad9629a33b4fdbbbc753efcd8.svg)\n\nTo compute $-5\\mathbf{a},$ we multiply each of the components by $-5\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\cdot a & =-5(2i+3j) \\\\  & =(-5\\cdot 2)i+(-5\\cdot 3)j \\\\  & =-10i+-15j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting our answer in angle bracket and column vector notations, we get \n![](images/05/05f847a73201db3de20db3847ef4ed97e07ea7fae9325401b1ea5a460968f9da.svg)"] [17592186060911 :tutorial/content "We just saw that the magnitude of the vector $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle -2,4 \\rangle$ was given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\,| & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, given a vector\n![](images/10/10fbddb6dc3528629e5583ad25976d049ca2ec5943ce3e689953f79299b8afcd.svg)\nthe magnitude of the vector can always be computed as\n\n$$\n|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| = \\sqrt{a_x^2+a_y^2}.\n$$\n\n**Note**\n\nWe sometimes use the notation $|| \\mathbf a ||$ to denote the magnitude of a vector."] [17592186060922 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & ={a}_{1}i+{a}_{2}j+{a}_{3}k \\\\ b & ={b}_{1}i+{b}_{2}j+{b}_{3}k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe cross product $\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf b$ can be calculated using the following determinant:\n\n![](images/b6/b615b60176ed4dedf45d4480b5a4da87bd31ca2aec5a917a26a2d798abfeb477.svg)\n\nFor instance, suppose that we have the two vectors $\\mathbf{a} = \\langle 2,-1,5 \\rangle$, $\\mathbf{b} = \\langle -1,4,2 \\rangle$. We can find the cross product of $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot 2-5\\cdot 4)i-(2\\cdot 2-5\\cdot (-1))j+(2\\cdot 4-(-1)\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =-22i-9j+7k \\\\  & =⟨-22,-9,7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186060933 :tutorial/content "We can calculate the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ using the following five-step algorithm.\n\n**Step 1:** Find the determinant $\\det(A)$ of the matrix.\n\n**Step 2:** Form the **matrix $M$ of the minors** of $A,$ where each entry $a_{ij}$ of $A$ is replaced by its minor $M_{ij}\\mathbin{:}$\n   ![](images/14/14905267240a1e8eb7f54ae47b813c24aa5eb26415c419bbd0ec53425693309b.svg)\n\n**Step 3:** Create the **matrix $C$ of cofactors** by changing the sign of some entries of the matrix of the minors according to the **rule of alternating signs**:\n   \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}+ & - & + \\\\ - & + & - \\\\ + & - & +\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFor each entry of $M$, we change the sign if the corresponding sign in the pattern is \"$-$\". Otherwise, if the corresponding sign in the pattern is \"$+$\", then we don't change the entry.\n\nSo, the matrix of cofactors is given by\n\n![](images/a5/a57831b81711ce44dc46c2a3a91b18d8dbfe4078697805369097421351294d2e.svg)\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors, $C^T.$\n\n![](images/e9/e93df3291f387eaac0f6992b38e005f72b08b71b99c7cef8fe6a3b8f42662ebc.svg)\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix:\n   \n\n$$\nA^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{\\det ( A)}C^{T}\n$$\n\nIn other words, we divide each entry of the transpose of the matrix of cofactors by the determinant of the original matrix (which we computed in step 1).\n\nLet's see this in practice, in the next example."] [17592186060936 :tutorial/content "We can combine vertical and horizontal shifts and stretches to get new transformed sine and cosine curves. For example, let's draw the graph of the curve \n![](images/a0/a06c3d1d4a3474ab4e8756bf41d7718495c5978f3fc656bef7ba1ea5f6f379f4.svg)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of ![](images/dc/dc5febeafd17e45544875b7479eaf66ff4f56d739a1794b204aac280765ab860.svg)\n\n![](images/6d/6d3c6289759e7b34c6ec8490ea1add8af6b7d4cb02fb37b7669bd5d96e7d0ffd.png)\n\n- Then, stretch it parallel to the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis by a stretch factor of ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) to give ![](images/a5/a5cc13d6ecde342df38cd8e3c27f9078a1aa9922fe0630ce7e8bb91293303d48.svg)\n\n![](images/7c/7c35f875500803e5fb9ea094831a7535c0ca367125bb006b3999dc113988785d.png)\n\n- Next, shift to the **left** by ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) units to give ![](images/ec/ec2496e027e1946a4686735d28e85f87761615ab801ce4a0e6fd1a7ed450f108.svg)\n\n![](images/92/925c9b75b3215bc239486b25dd9234002b28f585dca8358ad07403a88cf0b94b.png)\n\n- Finally, shift it **down** by ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) unit to give ![](images/a0/a06c3d1d4a3474ab4e8756bf41d7718495c5978f3fc656bef7ba1ea5f6f379f4.svg)\n\n![](images/26/26030969bd8c6f275555866542f0cb6f1f2f410454e7dc60b407e586dda5e93a.png)"] [17592186060954 :tutorial/content "We can combine vertical reflections, stretches, and shifts to get new transformed trigonometric curves. For example, let's draw the graph of the curve\n\n![](images/99/99f8d7bb60d08d48e8c2d757944053ac893d4f8848d8bd9cfa30daed617a742e.svg)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of ![](images/dc/dc5febeafd17e45544875b7479eaf66ff4f56d739a1794b204aac280765ab860.svg)\n\n![](images/6d/6d3c6289759e7b34c6ec8490ea1add8af6b7d4cb02fb37b7669bd5d96e7d0ffd.png)\n\n- Then, stretch it parallel to the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis by a stretch factor of ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) to give ![](images/a5/a5cc13d6ecde342df38cd8e3c27f9078a1aa9922fe0630ce7e8bb91293303d48.svg)\n\n![](images/7c/7c35f875500803e5fb9ea094831a7535c0ca367125bb006b3999dc113988785d.png)\n\n- Next, reflect it over the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis to give ![](images/ca/caf66236d26007222af32f9c7a74de04b8abfdd0c5e85ebae3b2263dc2b76de9.svg)\n\n![](images/7d/7d1184b7bab8eb3dbd2c99c5e16094809d4cef09a73522c4fcdce4941df4307d.png)\n\n- Finally, shift it **down** by ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) unit to give ![](images/99/99f8d7bb60d08d48e8c2d757944053ac893d4f8848d8bd9cfa30daed617a742e.svg)\n\n![](images/aa/aaa0f414eff732b89a1c9c74ec0fe286210e3e1aea12063635e235d1b0c658a1.png)"] [17592186060967 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population with the unknown population mean $\\mu$ and *known* population variance $\\sigma^2.$ Then, for a given value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ a $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\left(\\overline{x} - z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}, \\: \\overline{x} + z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2},$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1).$ \n\nThe endpoints of our confidence interval are called **confidence limits** and are given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nEach part of the formula above has a name:\n\n- $\\overline{x}$ is an estimate of the population mean\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}$ is the standard error of the mean\n\n- $z_{\\alpha/2}$ is the corresponding $z$-score\n\n- $E = z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}$ is the margin of error\n\n![](images/58/581fc2da88e4419e78389a1b5eedb127f590641d0b7ee21c489ed85184795b13.svg)\n\nSo, our confidence interval can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\overset{―}{x}-[\\text{margin of error}], & \\,\\overset{―}{x}+[\\text{margin of error}]) \\\\ (\\overset{―}{x}-[\\text{[math]z[/math]-score}]\\cdot [\\text{standard error}], & \\,\\overset{―}{x}+[\\text{[math]z[/math]-score}]\\cdot [\\text{standard error}]) \\\\ (\\overset{―}{x}-{z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}, & \\,\\overset{―}{x}+{z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince computing a confidence interval amounts to finding the corresponding confidence limits, we will use the two terms interchangeably."] [17592186060977 :tutorial/content "The unit circle provides a convenient way to represent the sine and cosine of special angles in each quadrant. \n\nWe first recall the following:\n\n- The ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-coordinate of any point on the unit circle equals the *cosine* of the corresponding central angle, and\n\n- the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinate equals the *sine* of the corresponding central angle.\n\nLet's use the unit circle to list the special values of sine and cosine, starting with the first quadrant. First, we mark off the special angles in this quadrant.\n\n![](images/3a/3a0c6eb5bc286272ef67aee5ef0fa625a6f4e44635413ca43060efc9082b1848.png)\n\nNow, let's write down each point's ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)- and ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinates, corresponding to the cosine and sine of each point's central angle. In addition, we'll highlight the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinate at each point.\n\n![](images/6b/6bb6bb7cc95a17c605056e1e1e1ae27bc8cf2f124e419c5e6b9810a72b950508.png)\n\nBefore we move on, observe that if we rotate counterclockwise from the positive ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis, we see that the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinates at the special angles are given by the following sequence:\n![](images/ef/ef3998f9651e37dba31e1597a503577243def17e903dbeb5322ab434a4815b3c.svg) \n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/ae/ae75322c270db13d7ee5ba6079b236b9a18d463f74cc3e5ee70b6fe113d224e3.png)\n\nThere is a similar pattern for the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-coordinates (corresponding to the cosine of the central angle). The only difference is that now we go in reverse, from ![](images/26/2614c435bff188ded3027f2a8f1ea011b37b41a1d62d2ff4bae181ce9107b5c2.svg) to ![](images/f1/f12b2ec86446e22fc9d1c982c156c7d089a97fecca1670f416ecfd63cf5899d3.svg) as shown below \n\n![](images/2a/2af7f2c7cf14c017a545a4144ed88dffe7b7f7bfb6e13a2b6144552ef3cfaa6c.png)\n\nTake a moment to ensure you can reproduce this diagram on your own without looking. We'll add to it shortly."] [17592186060991 :tutorial/content "The most important distribution in all probability and statistics is the **standard normal distribution.** This distribution describes the **standard normal random variable**, usually denoted as ![](images/9c/9ca1aa3a1f43f4453e28e65cf838ed686bf4e72c5a78668fb7ff7ca85c4e49f2.svg)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- ![](images/2f/2ff8585a9cda3f4625f17d79f11a40484b5d11d3908b34b868581c0bfaf40db5.svg) is a **continuous random variable**. Unlike discrete random variables, normally distributed random variables can take any value in the interval ![](images/ff/fffcf74a2091cb9448aad75b366cee9e5b8b3bd2600a8ceec08e669118adbd5b.svg)\n\n- Continuous random variables are described using a continuous curve called the **probability density function** (or PDF). In the case of ![](images/f0/f048f472450bfb3e1a1e3ef76a02338b0879fc88c37a81615f64641de75f0fef.svg) its PDF is denoted ![](images/30/3019d229913464cf5e93fc43c8cdb1a8e973174023d10ea1d345cfb1335bac27.svg)\n\n- ![](images/c2/c2298a29bae4706e73396095897cad806f08f64b05b57550804266d2bb1d9bfa.svg) has the shape of a so-called **bell curve.** The graph of this function is shown below.\n\n![](images/a0/a0907f70d833fe7ce75ae904036b3cab9644e8fe119f91ccedf840bd7b3be549.png)\n\nThe graph has the following important properties:\n\n- The graph is symmetric about the vertical axis.\n\n- The total area under the curve adds up to ![](images/da/daae2fd3251c4ba175906af980d1c33d4db057c616509ec6a146307d64417228.svg)\n\nTo state that a random variable ![](images/2f/2ff8585a9cda3f4625f17d79f11a40484b5d11d3908b34b868581c0bfaf40db5.svg) follows a standard normal distribution, we write \n\n![](images/c1/c1a5b6740351318fb48420ebbed2f09086cd253fb88b802c8f6bf138640d75fa.svg)\n\nIn this notation, ![](images/15/156b31ede73de5301befe67f02745a0202f1806a62f9fdecc6fc9ee3f21117ba.svg) and ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) refer to the mean ![](images/75/75d2faaecd7646e08d87276399a471f8f713fbeda95e358e94410ac2e23b42a5.svg) and variance ![](images/04/04862e2dc7fc74a4f84dbfc83381f578f58b758ce8a99e0ee864d2182b12dfc2.svg) of ![](images/f0/f048f472450bfb3e1a1e3ef76a02338b0879fc88c37a81615f64641de75f0fef.svg) respectively. We'll learn more about this later."] [17592186060994 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the truth table for $P\\Rightarrow Q.$\n\n| $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,Q\\,$ | $\\, P \\Rightarrow Q \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{black} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{black} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{black} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{black} \\textrm T$ |\n\nWe consider a concrete example to build further intuition behind the truth values in this table.\n\nSuppose your teacher promises that *if* you score at least $90$ on the exam, *then* you will receive an A. In other words, the teacher has promised that a score of at least $90$ on the exam implies that you will receive an A: \n![](images/d4/d4f1e90fe7e41304a17495224494b8860951877c22c58905f59f4580d067469e.svg)\n\nNow, the teacher may or may not be lying. The implication is true if the teacher tells the truth and false if the teacher lies. \n\nThe following four situations are possible:\n\n1\\. You score $90$ and receive an A. In this case, the teacher has told the truth. They have kept the promise. $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\n2\\. You score $90$ and do *not* receive an A. In this case, the teacher has lied. They have broken the promise. $\\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\n3\\. You do *not* score $90,$ yet you receive an A. In this case, the teacher has told the truth! The promise does not apply because you did not score at least $90.$ $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\n4\\. You do *not* score $90$ and do *not* receive an A. Again, the teacher has told the truth. The promise does not apply because you did not score at least $90.$ $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\nEach of these situations corresponds to a row in the following truth table:\n\n| ![](images/eb/ebffdf1820c35f70b5ab8325025bd413889c37eb3daec640f273eb6046808182.svg) | $\\textrm{Receive an A}$ | ![](images/e9/e94a03764a9e6c587b69756d54780679195290cf9b19c12df187194a009fc880.svg) |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{black} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{black} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{black} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{black} \\textrm T$ |"] [17592186063915 :question/problem "Let $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_{100}$ be a random sample of size $n=100$ from a population with probability density function \n![](images/e6/e607981b964434737a0a26949bbd54946732f7a4ae5dafcbf555478e72581e55.svg)\nGiven that the population variance $\\sigma^2 = 0.5,$ calculate $P( \\overline{X}>1.9 ).$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $\\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.01 \\,$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, -1.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0655 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0643 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0630 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -1.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0793 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0778 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0764 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -1.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0951 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0934 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0918 \\,$ |"] [17592186063936 :question/worked-solution "Since $281$ is larger than $170,$ the answer will be negative.\n\nWe start by solving an easier problem:\n\n$$\n281 - 170\n$$\n\nNext, we carry out the subtraction:\n\n![](images/32/32ceb366008206a2d765e3507a6b1d1d60682c407e9eb26ea90d230159a0377a.svg)\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n281-170 = 111.\n$$\n\nHowever, since our answer must be negative, we need to change the sign of the answer above. \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n170 - 281 = -111.\n$$"] [17592186063941 :question/worked-solution "Here, we are dividing a negative number $\\color{red}(-15)$ by a positive one $\\color{blue}(3).$ So the result will be negative:\n![](images/d1/d1a8196b5a3bee794d65631eeef52c4c1e0196072ae4a73aa644cad0ef191925.svg)\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $15$ by $3$ without any signs: $15 \\div 3 = 5.$ Therefore, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-15)} \\div {\\color{blue}3} = {\\color{red}-5}.\n$$"] [17592186063942 :question/worked-solution "We have to divide a positive number $\\color{blue}(50)$ by a negative one $\\color{red}(-5).$ So the result will be negative:\n![](images/98/983fa6f525dec74ed6866abdba19f23e28e318208497cd351a473035907795e3.svg)\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $50$ by $5$ without any signs: $50 \\div 5 = 10.$ Therefore,  we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{50}} \\div {\\color{red}{(-5)}} = {\\color{red}{-10}}.\n$$"] [17592186063943 :question/worked-solution "We have to divide a negative number ${\\color{red}(-81)}$ by another negative number ${\\color{red}(-9)}.$ So the result will be positive:\n![](images/bc/bcbaff723e9bce7d0cd513fb879cbc0a9758c2c5d9d584f4c55aab9df25e46d3.svg)\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $81$ by $9$ without any signs: $81 \\div 9 = 9.$ Therefore,  we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-81)} \\div {\\color{red}(-9)} = {\\color{blue}9}.\n$$"] [17592186063970 :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate $m,$ so we will treat the other variables $E$ and $C$ as constants. Since $m$ is being multiplied by $C,$ we need to divide both sides of the equation by $C$:\n![](images/27/27683350a3eb25bd3962e12724a01f09350d4c62ba85afb137df8cff27992be7.svg)\nThus, $m = \\dfrac E C.$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $C\\neq 0.$"] [17592186064033 :question/problem "The function $g(x)$ is given by\n![](images/c6/c67122865cc8decbb3238cd70c1fe662bdbcf9f697bd8a8e594e4408b88a08c3.svg)\nIf $g(x)$ is continuous at $x=2,$ what is the value $b?$"] [17592186064034 :question/problem "The function $h(x)$ is given by\n![](images/d1/d113c9de6e0c7946ed9952a2a245f4ba0ebb627b74c4661a2a067216fe5099d8.svg)\n\nGiven that $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=0,$ what is the value of $c?$"] [17592186064118 :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined for every real number $x$ and is given by\n![](images/90/90209edc9ee3cc9c6c17b71427dc4dda0a14558a9df7b2a71dbe017073b9086a.svg)\nIf $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1,$ what is the value of $k?$"] [17592186065362 :tutorial/content "Consider a curve ![](images/44/44c838deb3fa8e3182fbd9d4a03f964dc2731730559d00328190e9259bf01762.svg) between two points ![](images/3e/3e2237237edc778a26679551fc079de01fa2f06e33c1b0d0ff10dcecfd652dd4.svg) and ![](images/7d/7d4a3afa2b4b35de29b1645ebd729dfba3c3788ea2a6b926766e63e74beda2be.svg) The **average rate of change** of ![](images/44/44c838deb3fa8e3182fbd9d4a03f964dc2731730559d00328190e9259bf01762.svg) on the interval ![](images/8e/8efa43e7c8129d56297418824f9aebeef655e3f4c9cb65e7bf4c0a11f9d19208.svg) is computed as follows:\n\n![](images/51/5140837a34b6942abdc884a0d386209085034eb33c1f5d45517851c83d7d2517.svg)\n\nGeometrically, the average rate of change represents the slope of the **secant line** which passes through ![](images/5b/5b153647416113d95b1b6813579e099ad6b074369406be0f29bd2be28c9b5090.svg) and ![](images/1d/1dee6af8b808414a323d63634c2b5cf071792c7797a9bd60d7dc9d40f6b457bc.svg)\n\n![](images/e9/e9f4b7534020d6f78efc8375da02e347aca305fafee09ed0186d32866f12c058.png)\n\n**Note:** In general, a secant is a line that intersects a curve at two or more distinct points."] [17592186065368 :tutorial/content "Remember that the derivative of a function ![](images/93/93f888a1ee0b7da5e29f442f803f1c0eaf73ad02f5eaa97e85bb8a35d607015b.svg) represents the slope of the tangent line to the curve ![](images/e2/e2ed4a9881134f510823ba710f835acf5720b866f0df86fa6cd24448524431d6.svg)\n\nFor example, consider the curve ![](images/2e/2eb046be7e52e5a72d29a1333f9cf483411f0a8d6d897fe53014cc1952c6f759.svg), shown below. If we want to compute the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point ![](images/3a/3a613cb22e7bfd4038442b830babc21756061f5b18d1b1969451c8403d2894a3.svg) then we compute ![](images/a5/a54bfe86ff259aa5b62b72de2f050ee0c2818056cbc4bd30385c5faab112f71d.svg) and evaluate ![](images/91/910d575ac485dde82a562406c34e5e80677b10d5fb0e38b3370558d22badc908.svg)\n\n![](images/63/63f675532755376a733f2f0cad2b40e55f19b3278c1bcc5e8cb355fc6bdac277.png)\n\nIf ![](images/01/01ced1892dd48d3cbba7806664f3a1d763ef692dd88e5bf031d84db220141410.svg) then ![](images/a9/a9460f8b0abe06f33ff168a2a1887572f1c282de78c982c3af65c1c0670b9818.svg) by the power rule. Substituting ![](images/df/df9822243c11ec9f0fabca5cc578976d0bdea2c521b4f61964f5f3850a970504.svg) into ![](images/a5/a54bfe86ff259aa5b62b72de2f050ee0c2818056cbc4bd30385c5faab112f71d.svg) gives\n![](images/e0/e046ab7a11915ca797bd74e8e304484fe46ecaf4d2f19429409a4a8c6a3b1456.svg)\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve ![](images/2e/2eb046be7e52e5a72d29a1333f9cf483411f0a8d6d897fe53014cc1952c6f759.svg) at the point ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is ![](images/27/270d33581e0657a10d567c6cfaa529521b34e20a39c9a29550698cbf252fabfa.svg)"] [17592186065386 :tutorial/content "Suppose that Bob, who is ![](images/ae/ae1e193fbd577e9009d2601d3f65608af0827a16092c4046543cdb1ccddb9f74.svg) tall, stands in front of a ![](images/a7/a78087a5302fc6677b6ef30a50cfb0b170e3f51b10c25027c0427b938ad21148.svg) street light. How fast does the length of Bob's shadow change if he walks *away* from the street light with a speed of ![](images/0c/0cfc62307d856dbbed71500139ffd3427d49977f140a921ec2f53adb8804b61c.svg)\n\n![](images/0b/0b1f84cc2e31c1a7bdcf2663d8d82f4fecab7dfd12990041b2ba7fd0fe4174bf.png)\n\nWe can solve problems like this using three simple steps.\n\n**Step 1:** Draw a diagram and identify the variables that change with time.\n\n![](images/8d/8d80efb54f0c0f025eaec3cf02f4a1a40cb5a47f1dec4d40e1af63c8e4ac047f.png)\n\nIn the above diagram, we have denoted the following:\n\n- ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) is the distance between Bob and the street light\n\n- ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg) is the length of Bob's shadow\n\nBoth ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) and ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg) change with time as Bob walks away from the light. In particular, we're told that Bob's velocity is given by\n\n![](images/16/1608be1f575a84b2c86188a18b6a1eec623a97e1d4bf5534e092b8518ba5f837.svg)\n\nNotice that ![](images/63/632daac2c47092ffbca309417f73fd9d1a3385167c025ca07ca5a19c5632b294.svg) is positive. This is because the distance between Bob and the street light increases as Bob walks away from the light.\n\nOur goal is to calculate ![](images/89/89989044f19d9ed88641aeb7c0c0d0ce32aff15a2088eedbcc8944c9631ba3df.svg) \n\n** Step 2:** Write down an equation that relates ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) and ![](images/33/3337bbda5af4a3203ef0f6135b36de4f06c1915c4a3ff1bd5358326627c1eecd.svg)\n\nFrom our simplified diagram, we see that we have two right triangles that share a common angle. Therefore, they are similar by the AA (angle-angle) criterion for similar triangles. \n\nSince the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal. So, we have\n\n![](images/0c/0c73e5832167c46e7cbe124decb8968c19212f433bdc307e7653af2298f88446.svg)\n\n** Step 3:** Differentiate with respect to time and plug in the known values.\n\nWe use the chain rule to differentiate the above equation with respect to time. This gives\n\n![](images/21/219666475eed9f3f69895ef00a6c843c54c5b40a16b04060b6e4f988373bf694.svg) \n\nFinally, substituting the known value of ![](images/63/632daac2c47092ffbca309417f73fd9d1a3385167c025ca07ca5a19c5632b294.svg) gives\n\n![](images/85/858a9792dd0056bec55459ba9071aee83964102be336cc2fe797ab59f08253be.svg)\n\nTherefore, the length of Bob's shadow increases at a rate of ![](images/94/94cc677a1f2f2b5f1ee25d1290d932b6404e2b840e536c603eb373cc509857c1.svg)"] [17592186065391 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $f(x)$ below.\n![](images/20/20b2fd99ae051c3447e4c3530fee053b248393147a8295d3a9451524d7534f52.svg)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ seems to have a point discontinuity at $x=4.$ However, by selecting a particular value for $a,$ we can remove the point discontinuity and make the function continuous.\n\nWe can find this value of $a$ by forcing the limit of the function to be equal to the value of the function at $x=4\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 4} f(x) = f(4)\n$$\n\nRemember that $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 4} f(x)$ represents the value that $f(x)$ approaches as $x$ gets closer and closer, but not equal, to $4.$ So, when evaluating $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 4} f(x),$ we need to use the function definition for $x \\neq 4.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 4}(3x+a) \\\\  & =3(4)+a \\\\  & =12+a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $x=4,$ the function is defined as $f(x)=13.$ So $f(4)=13,$ and we can solve the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x) & =f(4) \\\\ 12+a & =13 \\\\ a & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to make $f(x)$ continuous at $x=4,$ we need to choose $a=1.$"] [17592186065402 :tutorial/content "Remember that to test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n1\\. Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n2\\. Does $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n3\\. Does $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x) = f(c)?$\n\nLet's use these three conditions to show that the following function is continuous at $x=1.$\n\n![](images/08/08f087f1b8c19fa8b8628bc1e3e35d3423cf54961b64832bdab64f8cbe298ece.svg)\n\nWe check all three conditions in turn.\n\n1\\. Using the expression for the first branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n  $$\nf(1)= 1+1= 2.\n$$\n\n  So, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1.$ $\\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n2\\. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n  $$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(1+x) \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(3-x) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  As a result:\n\n  $$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1^-} f(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1^+} f(x)\n$$\n\n  Therefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ exists: \n\n  $$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 1} f(x) = 2 \\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\n3\\. We have that $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 1} f(x) = 2 = f(1).$  $\\:\\: {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1.$ The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/c5/c55aa89d279d94b7561f6c24ebdb37bafcf46dbc20edf4f93d515aca7ae4cde6.png)"] [17592186065413 :tutorial/content "It is impractical to use number lines to subtract large numbers. \n\nHowever, a neat trick makes subtracting a positive number from a smaller positive number very easy!\n\nAs an example, let's consider the following subtraction problem:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{37}} - {\\color{blue}{68}}\n$$\n\nSince $68$ is larger than $37,$ the answer *must be negative*.\n\nWe start by solving an easier problem: subtracting the numbers *in the reverse order*. In our case, the reverse problem is\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{68}} - {\\color{red}{37}}.\n$$\n\nNext, we carry out the subtraction:\n\n![](images/db/db192e5c1f089e3d4cc2a7b1238392e44a7ba36bff99345a997042c16ebc304f.svg)\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{68}} - {\\color{red}{37}} = 31.\n$$\n\nHowever, since our answer must be negative, we need to change the sign of the answer above. \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{37}} - {\\color{blue}{68}} = -31.\n$$\n\nAnd that's it! \n\nLet's use the same idea to subtract two three-digit numbers."] [17592186065416 :tutorial/content "Dividing with negative numbers follows similar rules as multiplying with negative numbers. \n\nFirst of all, a negative number divided by a positive number gives a negative number. We can picture this rule as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-)\\,÷\\,(+)\\,=\\,(-).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, let's use this rule to find the value of $(-6) \\div 2.$\n\nHere, since we are dividing a negative number $\\color{red}(-6)$ by a positive number ${\\color{blue}(2)},$ the result is going to be negative:\n![](images/b6/b64108fa03fc286ddcd04ac4cd7a9d77c1672d0ced66ebb1953c97afa5488533.svg)\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $6$ by $2$ *without* any signs: $6 \\div 2 = 3.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-6)} \\div {\\color{blue}2} = {\\color{red} -3 }.\n$$"] [17592186065417 :tutorial/content "A positive number divided by a negative number is also a negative number. We can picture this rule as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(+)\\,÷\\,(-)\\,=\\,(-).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor instance, to find $8\\div (-2),$ we have to divide a positive number $\\color{blue}(8)$ by a negative one ${\\color{red}(-2)}.$ So the result will be negative:\n![](images/79/797eda7126fff569fccda2681c628f99f103057d7c5e23c1b65cb566b48ef203.svg)\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $8$ by $2$ without any signs: $8 \\div 2 = 4.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}8} \\div {\\color{red}(-2)} = {\\color{red}-4}.\n$$"] [17592186065418 :tutorial/content "Finally, the third rule is that a negative number divided by a negative number is a positive number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-)\\,÷\\,(-)\\,=\\,(+)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find $-12 \\div (-3).$ We have to divide a negative number $\\color{red}(-12)$ by another negative number ${\\color{red}(-3)}.$ So the result will be positive:\n![](images/d3/d31776de6ab95f8178d0bdd6e3b34cfa2f4fc0b987851dcace50aff1818936da.svg)\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $12$ by $3$ without any signs: $12 \\div 3 = 4.$ Therefore,  we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-12)} \\div {\\color{red}(-3)} = {\\color{blue}4}.\n$$"] [17592186065425 :tutorial/content "Subtracting a negative number is the same as adding a positive number. To remember this, just think that whenever you see a minus ($-$) sign followed by a negative ($-$) sign, they combine into a single plus $(+)$ sign, like so:\n![](images/54/54cf3d5630d0f6f43570b1c290019e92d035acaaac13b43ac40b7e3b093f988b.svg)\n\nA minus sign followed by a negative sign can be hard to make out, so it's considered better form to place parentheses around the negative number. For example, $9-(-4)$ is the same thing as $9 + 4.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\,\\overbrace{-\\,(-}^{+}\\,4) &= 9\\,+\\,4 \\\\ &= 13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \t\t- 16 -(-5) &= - 16 + 5.\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the result of adding $5$ to $-16,$ we start at $-16$ and move $5$ places to the right:\n\n![](images/fa/fa636855c7ec14299ddcb750b21561799f2b76848d52ee492f0a868cd77bccf0.png)\n\n \n\nWhen we do this, we land at $-11$. Therefore, \n\n$$\n-16-(-5)=-16+5=-11.\n$$"] [17592186065439 :tutorial/content "To expand an expression like   \n\n![](images/cd/cd1928f00c75236e8749153ea82e10efd3fc88d435eb095a940215337086e8a9.svg)  \n\nwe can use the distributive law. We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:  \n\n![](images/22/225564303e86ce4bce242c66c78434d94114de43c144933d6cadda2b718cfbc9.svg)"] [17592186068459 :question/worked-solution "To evaluate, we substitute $t = \\pi$ into the function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\pi ) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,\\,\\pi ⟩=⟨-1,0,\\pi \\,⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the value of $\\mathbf f (t)$ at $t = \\pi$ is $\\langle -1, 0, \\pi \\rangle.$\n\nWe can also express the answer using $\\mathbf i$-$\\mathbf j$-$\\mathbf k$ notation or square bracket notation, as follows:\n![](images/d9/d9ed4c8dcfbbc0fe7b4bf58e58197598221fc730613005a719252bf88c07d3f4.svg)"] [17592186068466 :question/problem "Factor ![](images/a0/a026f57159735d92a77f9fe46f17720e60e27081143e9fa0e128a69ca1e56687.svg)"] [17592186068466 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is negative. To reduce the expression to a more familiar form, we can factor out ![](images/66/66ef4b5796888b4025ee2d8b3d516ec80741b341db9389696b6e77a4af5208d8.svg) as follows:\n\n![](images/19/1950433407a5924a7809709ff5239c25e9132f229b4aa157d0af383d1f08b30c.svg)\n\nNow, we factor the quadratic expression inside the parentheses using the square of a difference formula:\n\n![](images/ba/baa2bc75c152c9a312cd1c09da0ac322782577892fc11679d8b5277a38ab627f.svg)\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n![](images/a0/a0fc84a06635785af990620d5d671329588a8018c0e0cdbc6beb130a23596070.svg)"] [17592186068654 :question/worked-solution "We factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any factors that they have in common.\n![](images/82/82b6ffda3164ec24147f28968a08f6d96bf64a2ec9aaf261f673c171dd1b1f40.svg)"] [17592186069921 :tutorial/content "Consider the plane region bounded by the curve ![](images/da/da9e7c622c9bb4816126baaf7030000b0df743977b620a20454f6f41974d4a66.svg) the curve ![](images/34/344c03d67f3618b1dc0560c072fc86443bf8eb991de0eed6ed1196fe20586053.svg) and the horizontal line ![](images/51/5105b7bf189fe285a6ab74d0f01cdea25d5acb40cac53551b0ac58def8d4180f.svg) as shown below.\n\n![](images/5c/5c3d071079a6f434bf7646a0fedef9d8670cf4d4d3809b6e2b42c5f2ba6833cd.png)\n\nWe rotate this region ![](images/6f/6f3e3ccd469bd4b7cf2baf7a17cad64bb11f3f6f2d202e79362b8c44df1fcdd2.svg) about the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis to get a solid of revolution, as shown below. How can we find the volume of this solid?\n\n![](images/67/67e20ea4be60086726fa1dfbede3d378f9b73e4ec57f8af722e8cebf307cd385.png)\n\nTo find the volume of this solid, we can use the same washer method approach that we used earlier for the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis. The only difference is that we need to swap ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) and ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg) in our formula.\n\nSo, to find the volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by the outer curve ![](images/fb/fb5fe1228a41ca0e72e76733ab39448868766741ac776c45aecea72e44f44f90.svg) the inner curve ![](images/5c/5c619f7c19342f727ea87a18b5934867e3e338a4fc0d8257d5943a585274f9b7.svg) and the horizontal lines ![](images/f8/f82a3483d5e6e5035663e40a89075138ad85d12dfd69b61d1f3bc611ca189e2e.svg) and ![](images/4d/4d9c2e8f5a6bb7f7f3c2e807701829a3f5e75f21549c3c9381d8d56e8ce7e33a.svg) is rotated ![](images/10/10745a5addeb479a769635882ff8e37e42bbc2998b149f0e57506bdc8e77915b.svg) radians about the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis, we use the following formula:\n\n![](images/a0/a079fcfafdd8b3a07003ef6cae69801526bfb6906129cd9349cd44d55f5c01cc.svg)\n\nThis is the same washer method as before, the only difference is that we need to swap ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) and ![](images/33/3337bbda5af4a3203ef0f6135b36de4f06c1915c4a3ff1bd5358326627c1eecd.svg) It involves breaking up the region into many thin washers (one such washer is shown below). \n\n![](images/9d/9d68e0672b26293e97fd8f8b2fbae4b4e3f743799d26a24291a3fd13075763d2.png)\n\nThe cross-sectional area of each washer corresponds to the region between two discs of radius ![](images/b9/b9f0db1d3432899cc481800eb60264a89a79ed6b611df8154be3fe42c37283d9.svg) and ![](images/0a/0a6417d7b72bf8af441e07e5b6ab87c0a7c6fb55520d22ab9bc175784595d811.svg) so the area of each washer cross-section is \n\n![](images/be/becabc91e909a4378a952ea7093d3e41329bf69861125d53f934bf346bbfe3f1.svg)\n\nand this is what we integrate.\n\nIn our example, the outer curve is ![](images/02/02e779dd5a005a08c5d479b17e3b9fda2f5c96f0483bba826a722f379b8c9c59.svg) and the inner curve is ![](images/34/344c03d67f3618b1dc0560c072fc86443bf8eb991de0eed6ed1196fe20586053.svg) so the volume is given by\n\n![](images/8b/8b555bb0836cb4a4a237faf7e1238332db6dbb5c9c8799e6db28b7ffb72c0cf1.svg)\n\nwhich simplifies to\n![](images/f4/f4bc30c392b233cc455162bb29c01f96c06aad15c5c6e3111cca35c32b4e6f12.svg)\n\nEvaluating this integral gives\n\n![](images/8e/8e82f449a8685fac34a3800371b4de23c09aa830fa80fe8d8140e7a618372221.svg)"] [17592186069922 :tutorial/content "For a parametrically defined function ![](images/e1/e159a3e09b9558743ad31676b2b24ddc5693567fa5f14a0db26b59ed056e218e.svg) we know that the derivative is given by\n\n![](images/b1/b19bbbdffd7fc7704f2f449afaa51a736b8dfd4cec0947e2e890cac9b990c390.svg)\nwhere ![](images/37/37108c872e8d79d8cfbccc54ca92b4cc53c2c31952db5169866b625862ba03ed.svg) denotes ![](images/7f/7f4ab03a7abb27a7d1c7ec192739835b48a83499d29e980b3114c77644a445da.svg) and ![](images/f1/f174e41615895d39c258b8ce122e1aa12f028de8f0da695c7449df90d29795b0.svg) denotes ![](images/44/4472311979bb886e78126f5a764202c0059808f2f02f5255d6b8ecd053a62edb.svg)\n\nTo calculate the second derivative ![](images/bd/bd5e402ed5bf31cdb9a7ead27e347b1a4df672818e8735051c778920439d13ef.svg) we differentiate ![](images/b9/b9e4e8135d478f59edec90e8937fb24436bc2054a40d9394ba589549e2c381ee.svg) with respect to ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) and use the chain rule, as follows:\n![](images/6d/6d67811848f344c1c55cc088ee3245f26c0481c596215be2637a40d57da24332.svg)\n\nFor example, let's calculate ![](images/ce/cea6f9b770105df62b1b9859c3058c6956cd0cfe829ab0ab11930626614d0d66.svg) for the curve defined by the parametric equations\n![](images/f1/f101347e55e3692c29df0116f7558cde59edfa31aac4a4332ae867ce48228ed8.svg)\n\nTo calculate ![](images/78/786b4218e2e979c2b578386a5346807871f0c5e0dbdc5b447a0013456d1fb51a.svg) we first need to compute the derivatives of ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) and ![](images/33/3337bbda5af4a3203ef0f6135b36de4f06c1915c4a3ff1bd5358326627c1eecd.svg) We get\n![](images/a2/a205363ef2cdbad433205b0989610454dfc3171ba8fc877bcd93efcb8f220dcf.svg)\n\nSo, we have\n![](images/e4/e4040e12f12318a4f76fadf15526554498bea42cb7909e3d371c778a63d4b380.svg)\n\nNow, computing the second derivative, we get\n\n![](images/6c/6cb5b12a7ea832c72b9e74cec5e4b7aea36bef4df98e493b1258253d2154d483.svg)"] [17592186069953 :tutorial/content "The graph of  ![](images/78/7816c015ff36e92e579c157d0d1e074270678c48b06f0c2d37d920311224d5e5.svg) looks like the standard parabola ![](images/2e/2eb046be7e52e5a72d29a1333f9cf483411f0a8d6d897fe53014cc1952c6f759.svg), but shifted ![](images/6c/6cb44f25e489afb06ec62d2e95c4d235ece77eb36e2327b856264d2fb8b22c21.svg) units to the *right* along the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis. \n\n![](images/a4/a48db73aadd4192f244676c3cb54c6849ca284acf1adbf29d6245baa5a07afcb.png)\n\nSimilarly, the graph of ![](images/ce/ce16aa1f1cc35aaeae8aba5c19c0ebf1e9ea43e9624f0c6c18b42f7bfa21c304.svg) looks like the standard ![](images/2e/2eb046be7e52e5a72d29a1333f9cf483411f0a8d6d897fe53014cc1952c6f759.svg) parabola after it's been shifted ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) units to the *left*:\n\n![](images/9c/9c33ae042e23cee23f96a35f31afbb3f80fb1dfece702b26c2a1aded17885861.png)\n\nIn general, the graph of ![](images/4b/4b00aba256457d4efcd4f517482795d922665890adf0c2f0c0991527b5c1edfb.svg) looks like the standard parabola ![](images/2e/2eb046be7e52e5a72d29a1333f9cf483411f0a8d6d897fe53014cc1952c6f759.svg) but translated ![](images/99/994427fe929fdf538d7c1be792e6fa4b85697f0921161bf5ddf0e639864c9f27.svg) units right. The vertex of ![](images/4b/4b00aba256457d4efcd4f517482795d922665890adf0c2f0c0991527b5c1edfb.svg) is located at ![](images/ef/efb3fb22e5e729a56d75a55b8e4b43e7ebf0cb77a3798550afdf9e05ac3a1525.svg)\n\n**Watch Out!** It's easy to confuse the translations.\n\n- For ![](images/78/7816c015ff36e92e579c157d0d1e074270678c48b06f0c2d37d920311224d5e5.svg), we subtract ![](images/6c/6cb44f25e489afb06ec62d2e95c4d235ece77eb36e2327b856264d2fb8b22c21.svg) from ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) inside the parentheses, and the parabola moves to the right, *not* to the left (even though it seems like it should be the other way around).\n\n- Likewise, for ![](images/ce/ce16aa1f1cc35aaeae8aba5c19c0ebf1e9ea43e9624f0c6c18b42f7bfa21c304.svg), we add ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) to ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) inside the parentheses, and the parabola moves to the left, *not* to the right."] [17592186069978 :tutorial/content "To sketch the graph of ![](images/0d/0d56960f9390e8a7fe5c1fe17b9fa8b62fe9f33e052eb295161ce7a368047111.svg) we start with the graph of ![](images/f3/f39b65736426b97929738170b1a75a6aac5ba9cfbad1b444a65098297b7b1d63.svg) and stretch it by a scale factor of ![](images/6c/6cb44f25e489afb06ec62d2e95c4d235ece77eb36e2327b856264d2fb8b22c21.svg) parallel to the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis, as shown below.\n\n![](images/d0/d01107a224cc4a12f05263a7f159c8ad25a04ff061e1eda3045113e6671f0113.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The point ![](images/0c/0c733476e5ce2ef628b2dd5447582a6e69de8d045147925a803dcfb6e9834a0c.svg) on ![](images/f3/f39b65736426b97929738170b1a75a6aac5ba9cfbad1b444a65098297b7b1d63.svg) is mapped to the point ![](images/c8/c8d810917eef2cc68b3c133405d6aa3fe648c64b6f8e50cc1a0bcc4eb84ba536.svg) on ![](images/62/62dc824c36f1634a45236f35fa955f7b7863996ea26708e7e4295d0a73e258be.svg)\n\n- The point ![](images/c1/c18723d4a2788ff033587f5be97e6a0b50d886a8f74a130c92eb348d2a6498b9.svg) on ![](images/f3/f39b65736426b97929738170b1a75a6aac5ba9cfbad1b444a65098297b7b1d63.svg) is mapped to the point ![](images/c9/c9629cfe801a15e75ff1548b053d4507fc7bff739ce77315e64cf5bb7fd6bd21.svg) on ![](images/62/62dc824c36f1634a45236f35fa955f7b7863996ea26708e7e4295d0a73e258be.svg)"] [17592186069980 :tutorial/content "Adding a value to ![](images/2e/2eb046be7e52e5a72d29a1333f9cf483411f0a8d6d897fe53014cc1952c6f759.svg) will **translate** (shift) the parabola up or down the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis. For instance, adding ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) to ![](images/2e/2eb046be7e52e5a72d29a1333f9cf483411f0a8d6d897fe53014cc1952c6f759.svg) will move each point on the parabola ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) units up.\n\n![](images/2a/2a233fc5fe21dd3ffd460ab3fa343ac3c4587bca17fd958343309bc723a2cda5.png)\n\nOn the other hand, subtracting ![](images/6c/6cb44f25e489afb06ec62d2e95c4d235ece77eb36e2327b856264d2fb8b22c21.svg) will move each point of the parabola ![](images/6c/6cb44f25e489afb06ec62d2e95c4d235ece77eb36e2327b856264d2fb8b22c21.svg) units down.\n\n![](images/5b/5bcc36e857503b3faf90d1cbb58750150dc7849a0f2a0b3c58b77319ffff1224.png)\n\nIn general, the graph of ![](images/00/00abc9adb93347077819fc66fecc1e7eef09e68fc6f8e94a76e27c8eb70b13a0.svg) looks like the standard parabola ![](images/2e/2eb046be7e52e5a72d29a1333f9cf483411f0a8d6d897fe53014cc1952c6f759.svg) but translated ![](images/8d/8dc6f7e80ecaac9fa07890ce1881dd51de319597ae8b1dce821aa03c1f3eff06.svg) units up. The vertex of ![](images/00/00abc9adb93347077819fc66fecc1e7eef09e68fc6f8e94a76e27c8eb70b13a0.svg) is located at ![](images/e8/e8c4c8598a8228afb25cbf13c034e2fe234393f9b9c0dfde8be1726b4fc79a8e.svg)"] [17592186069989 :tutorial/content "One of the advantages of using the shell method versus other methods is that the resulting integral can be much simpler to evaluate. This is often true when we want to rotate a curve of the form $x = f(y)$ about the $x$-axis.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to compute the volume generated when the region below is rotated about the $x$-axis:\n\n![](images/1d/1d23dde2cdd557feb50f82f72f4ff09174d5fb9a0c94e7810a6c06acb95a13d0.png)\n\nAccording to the shell method, the volume $V$ is given by the following straightforward integral: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(2y-2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}2{y}^{2}-2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now compare this to the disc method.\n\nTo determine this volume using the disc method, we'd first need to express our curve as two separate functions of $x,$ one for the lower part and one for the upper part, as follows:\n\n![](images/d9/d957a8e263db8ddd8ad3e5fa37f63b0cdbb47e706c367350c3ff6b8ff90294ff.svg)\n\n![](images/f4/f416ab43297265aec4836eca2020d8f08976852cc025ad4d3a0766023187adef.png)\n\nThe required volume is equal to the difference between the volumes generated when the upper and lower curves are rotated about the $x$-axis. Thus, according to the disc method, the required volume is given by\n\n$$\nV = \\pi \\int_{0}^{1/2} \\left( \\left[ \\dfrac{1}{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1-2x} \\right]^2 - \\left[ \\dfrac{1}{2}-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1-2x} \\right]^2 \\right) \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nAs we can see, this integral is a lot more complicated than the one we obtained using the shell method!"] [17592186070006 :tutorial/content "If ![](images/9a/9ac10abedf6cecc066a4858ab2859e3c3038a43b10ac3261bd4120d0dc861990.svg) on ![](images/88/88e5bb9f44a0b3bb16a7e0e5461c5cc3f1f0bef907eda396d725caf367f29fb5.svg) the area ![](images/6b/6bd69882ede929379fa9babdf49386dd09138f26cabc6a4d617bbac5e92d21a5.svg) under the curve ![](images/44/44c838deb3fa8e3182fbd9d4a03f964dc2731730559d00328190e9259bf01762.svg) over the interval ![](images/2f/2f2185d0962aceaafb8dbacf7486e61ccafbb77a54e961b33e886dd3b79a20df.svg) can be approximated using a left Riemann sum by calculating the area of each rectangle and adding them together.\n\n![](images/85/8547bd96f866100db3ed28f3f23e3446ab5eff50632c425c23c2916c89f823db.png)\n\n  \n\nWe can write this mathematically as\n\n![](images/c9/c92cf3627eeb4798f2f72577c3df4788c311b59b575ccbfa17caf14f39dacff1.svg)\n\nwhere ![](images/4a/4a4a2bd99240362f13e0954449f58ab1c21ae7d29ffec2a1b2c8f1350f6fbec7.svg) and ![](images/09/0979b76b241c9ddb5f2e3d097811c00175e0f9242fa36cc4bff45e2963c3dd8f.svg) are the endpoints of the interval, ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) is the number of rectangles, and the step size ![](images/bd/bd77198ba2d349a9ae25a0866060aa94624d6e255cabe04e818dc06e755ae85c.svg) is given by\n![](images/c9/c9f5c5a2741bae825236da3287d754a2526954e894011ace34f5cde5e65a4b45.svg)\n\nWe can simplify by factoring out the ![](images/a7/a7e281a725fb2bff766e9ddb72c85bb6f7a8e7d45de6212372795ba1764d5ddc.svg) which gives the following general formula:\n\n![](images/27/277114008d8847a523fb417695fe2f62a4f7ff08efc0b9b8ea58556029ea9c5e.svg)"] [17592186070007 :tutorial/content "In the last few examples, the step size $\\Delta x$ was the same for each of the rectangles. In these cases, we say that the step size is **regular**.\n\nThere are situations where the step size can vary, which we call an **irregular step size**. But we can still use a left Riemann sum to approximate the area.\n\nFor example, let's use a left Riemann sum to approximate the area under the curve of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ whose data is given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3.5$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $4$ | $6$ | $12$ |\n\nFirst, note that the domain of $f(x)$ has been *partitioned* as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n [1,4] &= [1, 2] \\cup [2, 3.5] \\cup [3.5, 4] .\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's plot what this function might look like, using the values given in the table. \n\n![](images/2e/2e8563480f46ff335042c3c52a6f60983f871dccb886c8cf3ca0844504a73db6.png)\n\n  \n\nWe've drawn some rectangles to help us calculate our left Riemann sum, and we have chosen the heights of each rectangle to be calculated using the left endpoint of each interval. \n\nBy calculating the area of each rectangle, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n![](images/8f/8f62020b1888f7479665b04028acb822fdccfa9049a37e306ea30ce0aa703ac7.svg)\n\nPlugging our numbers into the above, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal{A}\t&\\approx\t{\\color{red}1} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}2-1})\n\t\t\t\t\t\t+ {\\color{red}4} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}3.5-2})\n\t\t\t\t\t\t+ {\\color{red}6} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}4-3.5}) \\\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= {\\color{red}1} \\cdot {\\color{blue}1} +{\\color{red}4} \\cdot {\\color{blue}1.5}+ {\\color{red}6} \\cdot {\\color{blue}0.5}  \\\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&=1+6+3\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= 10.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186070011 :tutorial/content "We can also define vector-valued functions with three components. For example,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f} (t) = t \\ \\mathbf{i} + 2t^2 \\ \\mathbf{j} + 3t^3 \\ \\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nThis particular definition uses the so-called $\\mathbf{i}$-$\\mathbf{j}$-$\\mathbf{k}$ notation.\n\nWe can represent the same function using **angle bracket** notation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f (t) = \\langle t,  \\: 2t^2, \\: 3t^3 \\rangle\n$$\n\nAnother notation that's often convenient is **bracket** notation:\n\n![](images/a9/a95fd21b6ea5e7eae150b516d7d0ad82d283a31c895c9223abbeac81b622bb7d.svg)\n\nIt's important to realize that all three notations represent the same function."] [17592186073447 :question/worked-solution "All of the answer choices have the second number equal to $3.$ So our equivalent ratio is of the form ![](images/35/35577ae6ed7f13a59f140d75fbd066251ec539423a5de48daa1736e7bbec25f8.svg)  \n\nTo find a ratio that's equivalent to $12 : 9,$ we divide both numbers by the same value. In particular, we need to get $3$ from ${\\color{red}{9}},$ so we divide ${\\color{red}{9}}$ by $3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 &  & : & \\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,9 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\boxed{\\text{[math]\\phantom{0}[/math]}} &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,3 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n So to get ![](images/21/211a836d739a9a5eadbaa452ad307a4d01d32818d1bd2ccee48eec256f64a2f0.svg) from ${\\color{blue}{12}},$ we divide $12$ by $3$ as well:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}12} \\div 3 =4\n$$\n\nTherefore, $12:9$ is equivalent to $4:3.$ \n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [17592186073449 :question/problem "![](images/8d/8d9f0a004ca9beb84b3a2a604d71dbf43ab70a67d899b167c5183ecc2ebe15f3.png)\n\nWhat number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of squares to circles is ![](images/29/296da85a5c6d0f5cf14f8fe144b0cdf66e4082b425b886d98fcbe672a2e8a190.svg) because there is $1$ square for every ![](images/b1/b1fda7079b1445faec7a1a2bb2abcafd9147f8a28863a2618c66166cea102a14.svg) circle(s)."] [17592186073467 :question/problem "Each of the three shapes in the model below represents one whole.\n\n![](images/22/222af9ec253a6624ece646225db926069cd7be3ac416fe846a9216360981f8cb.png)\n\n \n\nFrom left to right, what numbers are missing from the division problem below?\n![](images/1f/1f6d1040a30647e11e455ec6f50ab4fd41e499b74a47b182495381e1bd92df14.svg)"] [17592186073467 :question/worked-solution "In the model, there are $\\color{red}3$ shapes. Each shape consists of $4$ pieces that each represent $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ of a whole. There are $\\color{blue}12$ pieces in total.\n\nThe model tells us that when we divide $\\color{red}3$ wholes into quarters, we will be left with $\\color{blue}12$ pieces.\n\nTherefore, the division problem is:\n![](images/4c/4ca736b0aa98a4104f9a427fe0dad68fbd171b84ce07d02c67a848fe69022724.svg)\n\nSo, the missing numbers are $\\color{red}3$ and $\\color{blue}12.$"] [17592186073558 :question/problem "What is missing in the following sentence?\n\nTo make a fertilizer Esther mixed $2$ quarts of fertilizer concentrate and $3$ quarts of distilled water, so there are ![](images/e6/e67bc79fa98ba44b9b1c5d5727182e936472f4b7d7b52967a54b069f178cf66d.svg) quarts of fertilizer concentrate for each quart of distilled water."] [17592186073559 :question/problem "From left to right, what are the missing numbers in the following sentence?\n\nA chef requires $\\dfrac{1}{7}$ grams of chilies for each gram of turmeric for his recipe. Therefore, the recipe has a ratio of ![](images/ad/ada4a53796b76bbb852b81d98119a002b3840b7274b34aa8a321e1fe849215ee.svg) grams of chilies to ![](images/0c/0ce2b8005d66f823fc23dcefcbe5881d8a8fd85b34e2d711214b13ed959d85e8.svg) grams of turmeric."] [17592186073570 :question/problem "What is the missing number in the statement below?\n\n$\\qquad$ $16$ chickens in $2$ chicken coops ![](images/63/63d89cadf68ca22a5e3e0d9f6477b44a5ceda512a9d09e5496ad60268191867c.svg) chickens per chicken coop."] [17592186074958 :tutorial/content "Fraction multiplication occurs regularly in real-life situations.\n\nFor example, suppose that a bag contains ![](images/bc/bcc2ab102a29294d71449d99af1cf1a88aae88047223f829175086f7a6cd5e10.svg) of flour. If Nancy used ![](images/c0/c0db422914dd04be9adf05af649ec06419e80d8425cd0a85f6105cef7cc447b5.svg) of the bag to make a pie, how much flour did she use to make the pie?\n\nTo find out how much flour Nancy used, we need to multiply ![](images/16/1627bb3010ada55ed941966d38081f1a6c1394c07ca78b94585f8c97e4bbc138.svg) by ![](images/d9/d989525f1462386565e344f7e9a41d48cea1e85148f9f5aba25268f589a8bcbc.svg)\n\nTo multiply these two fractions, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n![](images/58/58824e95f00448b5c7d509f0781a367506ccf37fa018f2a0ba373c74191f1bb6.svg)\n\nTherefore, Nancy used ![](images/fd/fd1a8efcb6ae625f2259a43e8ffeb93d486d409af6175b6e52380f4ccd488af7.svg) kilograms of flour."] [17592186075026 :tutorial/content "The shortest distance between a point and a line is the length of the line segment that connects the point to the line and is perpendicular to the line.\n\nFor example, consider the point ![](images/e2/e2002597cf3d8b01e24e7b4d2c6daf6fc9afb0afb3c6140877abfc3bf1be02c4.svg) and the horizontal line ![](images/06/06444505f504ecd01a91d4846b46bf202caafa9d9f756958483262f69b5db14b.svg) shown below:\n\n![](images/8c/8cba6df956e809981ac4a3ae78a6f6843be0ca8561fbcadd9d9ba98f2a5325cc.png)\n\nTo find the distance between the point and the line, we construct a segment with its endpoints on the point and the line, and which is perpendicular to the line\n\n![](images/80/802b30a788850457206716430755da2d5e3c6b3a53633c95626e49ee668d2507.png)\n\nThe distance between the point and the line is the length of this segment, which we compute as the absolute value of the difference in ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinates of the point and the line:\n\n![](images/11/11e3682c2b811da299d9c475bd0ba3684d0c7f68e5677d925744b37026c47b0c.svg)"] [17592186075035 :tutorial/content "Suppose that the following two ratios are equivalent:\n\n![](images/c6/c673bfcd099ef612f48bd0da7f8535cb9406d23088392e056884fb9415bb5100.svg)\n\nWhat is the missing number?\n\nTo find a ratio equivalent to $3 : 5,$ we multiply or divide both numbers by the same value. \n\nIn this case, we need to get $12$ from ${\\color{blue}{3}},$ so we multiply ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ by $4.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,3 &  & : & \\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,5 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓\\times \\,4 &  &  &  & \\,\\,↓\\times \\,4 &  \\\\ 12 &  & : &  & \\,\\boxed{\\text{[math]\\phantom{0}[/math]}} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to get ![](images/50/50f3c1b8dc3b1628130d1de11abcc455fc22006f32b7486f658c8134fcc3221c.svg) from ${\\color{red}{5}},$ we multiply ${\\color{red}{5}}$ by $4$ as well: \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}5} \\times 4 = \\bbox[3px,lightgray]{\\color{red}20}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $20.$"] [17592186075038 :tutorial/content "Let's take a look at the following equivalent ratios:\n\n![](images/6c/6cda61e0fbd0d9695644a29f0c6fa3478eae84cf253623da7f4b544f35873472.svg)\n\nWe can place the equivalent ratios in a **ratio table**, such as the one below. In a ratio table, each row represents a ratio.\n\n| ${\\color{blue}5}$ | ${\\color{red}10}$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| ${\\color{blue}10}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}\\textrm{?}}$ |\n| ${\\color{blue}20}$ | ${\\color{red}40}$ |\n\nOne number is missing. Let's figure out what it is.\n\nWe start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}5}:{\\color{red}10},$ which is the first ratio in the table. Writing this ratio as a fraction, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}5}}{{\\color{red}10}}.\n$$\n\nThe numerator represents the left-hand entry (${\\color{blue}5}$), and the denominator represents the right-hand entry (${\\color{red}10}$).\n\nTo find the missing number, we need to make a numerator of ${\\color{blue}10}.$ We can do this by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\times 2}{10\\times 2} & =\\frac{10}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now add this to our ratio table:\n\n| ${\\color{blue}5}$ | ${\\color{red}10}$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| ${\\color{blue}10}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}20}$ |\n| ${\\color{blue}20}$ | ${\\color{red}40}$ |\n\nSo, the missing number is $\\color{red}20.$"] [17592186075052 :tutorial/content "Remember that a *unit* fraction is a fraction containing ![](images/cc/ccee5f5be0445cce59832a3def74f395a7d05cc12dc630fb36baf8f3d2d448bd.svg) in the numerator. So, for example, the fractions \n\n![](images/2e/2e38b15a8f3be74e15b87e81264b66571e593de7250dc902aeaa0e7bc1c506bf.svg)\nare both unit fractions, whereas \n\n![](images/d1/d157ce836a54214740d295f545d4c94d8c57d54d92ac7f075ed9b92556c932b1.svg)\nare *not* unit fractions.\n\nIn this lesson, we will use models to divide unit fractions by whole numbers. \n\nLet's use a fraction model to find the value of \n\n![](images/d8/d8815c6716e962ce4aebee24d004d4c591a1bc35c5fdebcd87d26ace61ead7fb.svg)\nWe start with a square model of ![](images/9f/9fcebe90d169f37bbdbf55f9e4ac86ceaf6ea6d573d101f382a12008bb0c5c2a.svg) split *vertically*.\n\n![](images/cc/ccfe0f457342c456200c98cfc46e63bdc1153698282085164eeafae5c2092c45.png)\n\nTo divide ![](images/9a/9ae11798da63cafee6ce5ccf824a7b96f9f7407a4ee445d1431d907c36f5dd1b.svg) by ![](images/db/dbfe30ca4e672459c180bd90f25f84f025162b8ce73260773fb1fbdf39b0a052.svg) we split the above whole into ![](images/6f/6f33d8df0d08ae9bddce540bedf62f46e93e748e4a5b6fd85b1dc626953e44a6.svg) equal parts *horizontally*. Then, we remove any shaded parts that are not in the bottom row.\n\n![](images/79/7940073ba05d3136ee1d465530a60bc3624b7aa06fd7484d30797758a28976d7.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we obtain that the shaded part represents ![](images/42/42accbcb8cbfd14800ac4a27bc87d5ba529ff4173a91499f32705cd41b0c88b0.svg) of the whole.\n\nTherefore,\n![](images/f1/f14a0f0f9efb98bdfe6cf40f4b1bbb2c88dc20272caaf9d604fcc44bce79d53c.svg)"] [17592186078168 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$5 \\,\\mid\\, 6^n +4$ for all $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$\n\nWhat are the missing entries in the proof template outlined below?\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n![](images/a7/a78327c81dcdedb9aa41dc74ca699474c576eca38fc30dc1352fd5e813bd5642.png)\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some ![](images/d5/d515b3edf4446956ce045136a3c33f358eb1e733f0f5dbebd66bd29f7d39f8a1.png) we have that\n![](images/e0/e07dc9d411f59200318f64763282e20c72441b4ca0dfd6cf9290cd4fffbc7ecf.png)\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n![](images/e0/e07dc9d411f59200318f64763282e20c72441b4ca0dfd6cf9290cd4fffbc7ecf.png)\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, $5 \\,\\mid\\, 6^n +4$ for all $n\\in \\mathbb{N}.$"] [17592186078220 :question/problem "Given that the function\n![](images/f7/f72fd02a0488c44ac7472487222507c5739853cd25720502a726844e0c23c915.svg)\nis continuous at $x=0,$ find the value of the constant $b.$"] [17592186078222 :question/problem "Find the value(s) of the constant $a$ that make the function $f(x)$ below continuous at $x=2.$\n![](images/be/bee9ecfc2bcfaa7741ed1c2aca90b8a077c19715c2a642a9622856f986f434f3.svg)"] [17592186078225 :question/problem "Given that the function\n![](images/47/47e2bbdd0741bbac490c3ac0851e95b2103078e22d06d4a369712236529e8302.svg) \nis continuous at $x=1$, find the value of $a.$"] [17592186078309 :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^5 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\\,?$\n![](images/25/256fe45a207e24c746e9b00eb92fa6d32404d240df3916d1536968592f117700.png)"] [17592186078334 :question/problem "Find the value of the constant $c$ that makes the function $h(x)$ below continuous at $x=4.$\n![](images/6e/6ed78555d5936e679b9aecd838d8b07f95031afe831eb3ef3e7cb64c5160f7c1.svg)"] [17592186079612 :tutorial/content "We can often model real-life scenarios with a linear equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\text{(total value)} =  \\text{(starting value)} + \\text{(rate of change)}n.\n$$\n\nHere, **rate of change** means the same as **unit rate.**\n\nTo illustrate, suppose that Emily has ![](images/e2/e2e07b8b49a865b90f630cc52db01422d09e3777edd23d7f457beca6c9086b0a.svg) in savings, and she takes a job that pays ![](images/7b/7beab0f2f12ccbc183cfce2c066d3df3f9dfdd95edcd20824f0dfa80c2e73d83.svg) per day.\n\nAfter $1$ day, the total amount ($T$) of dollars that she has is\n\n$$\nT = 150 + 100(1).\n$$\n\nAfter $2$ days, the total amount of dollars that she has is\n\n$$\nT = 150 + 100(2).\n$$\n\nFollowing this pattern, after $n$ days, the total amount of dollars that she has is\n\n$$\nT  = 150 + 100n.\n$$\n\nWe can interpret this equation as follows:\n\n- $150$ represents the *starting value* because Emily started with ![](images/79/79753800e43de48c8d1f9299c25866c652acac7691806d1f70aee26071cba9de.svg) in savings.\n\n- $100$ represents the *rate of change* because Emily earns ![](images/ba/ba59b309a5eca5f891b8074903ef5949200dc5ff4d44bc4d27b81bb612ca12fa.svg) *per day.*"] [17592186079640 :tutorial/content "Using a calculator, we can evaluate the sine of the angle ![](images/17/17aa8e3ab57c18aa2d1a6d04653701085d3beec29c4805e9d6e18c22d586e466.svg) and get \n![](images/5c/5c5e85144e6e1aab226ee174adbb30a7edbe80bb9f8a5770d6205e81a4f6cc77.svg)\n\nBut what if we are given the value ![](images/6a/6a2e70809b6bc7130f042930e4e9a611ffafbf828c38d967ee5cf48d04d3ba72.svg) and we want to find the angle whose sine is this value? To do this, we can use the **inverse trigonometric functions**.\n\nIn the case of the sine, its inverse is **arcsine** (written as ![](images/19/1938ea6d9f1fa40ed0d039861e7c701e9ba3127c806c5a8eb7c1000b8ec5f941.svg) or ![](images/12/12f3b6602187e07917c7d4a7824afcd5a426c276a0065a780e6bc3bc84dd7411.svg)). If we apply arcsine to a sine value, it gives the corresponding angle:\n![](images/a5/a531b557f0d9668cbd2c5a55fdfcca2173e29faf4c7ed4a96bcc8ce76d8f23ce.svg)\n\nThe inverses of the other trigonometric functions work in the same way.\n\n- The inverse of sine is ![](images/39/39a3c758b61a3886b6945c0f29328934ad207c74ba713ef10770352ebbe69f32.svg)\n\n- The inverse of cosine is ![](images/9b/9b8a569b724129728610b4bd7ba28966ea13c064d08000075312f294724f4e45.svg)\n\n- The inverse of tangent is ![](images/a1/a111203310c10fdfa70deb7b5e7a2b1f6658310e4d13140ad84369beeaf46b19.svg)\n\nTry it for yourself. But make sure that your calculator is in \"degrees\" mode!\n\n**Note:** In this topic, we only deal with acute angles. Things get a little more complicated for non-acute angles, but you'll learn about that later!"] [17592186079675 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given the graph of ![](images/c9/c956e6cbfb2d94fa56a68a3173ec78f60fee33a9066c9c44ca4096d67d7b1cba.svg) shown below.\n\n![](images/2c/2cb61ee785ff6e04c5b73281389d6653f42bc93636da6840fbc2391ffb82451c.png)\n\nHow can we use graph transformations to plot the graph of\n\n![](images/c5/c546f9b96ef07d40050b7d98f046528f54ea43afc5d42c1bac48d2a02406e079.svg)\n\nSince this function involves several transformations, we apply them in the following order:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of ![](images/a0/a047a10a3ed3f5e86676e8fca21093abfe6a1849d90ce94b62101f34fca37e3d.svg) We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of ![](images/a0/a047a10a3ed3f5e86676e8fca21093abfe6a1849d90ce94b62101f34fca37e3d.svg) We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nIn this case, we begin by factoring out the coefficient next to the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) inside the function: \n\n![](images/e9/e905676d5234ac091212cf51bce10510cdde7e6c5000aabb564dfee38ca4c020.svg)\n\nNow, to plot ![](images/18/1867234b91fecd21c650bf6b29134a695d20150a98990c6c7b65ffed0a858a8a.svg) we apply the following steps:\n\n- Consider the curve ![](images/e2/e2ed4a9881134f510823ba710f835acf5720b866f0df86fa6cd24448524431d6.svg)\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of ![](images/9a/9ae11798da63cafee6ce5ccf824a7b96f9f7407a4ee445d1431d907c36f5dd1b.svg) parallel to the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis to get the graph of ![](images/a1/a142ed820c2bdafb8c1b7969028c7f4533c66fa772a73954c46f6b5eb91a050f.svg)\n\n![](images/07/07fe84bddbdc721dfba2fc9b720ca79ff794e9846fa1ef3bbc0c3c2217edd4e8.png)\n\n- Then, translate it to the right by ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) units to get the graph of ![](images/d0/d027e035caec097035ee8f20e49d28dadb156445265efe674460854cce152406.svg)\n\n![](images/a1/a16ccd8e56c64f80f93476f2b7a41aa3093a2e4b9fdbe089fa502d1f1df15923.png)\n\n- There is no vertical scaling, so we omit this step.\n\n- Finally, translate it up by ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) unit to get the graph of ![](images/f6/f6d6c8ff90e58f882bbd48d3b52f37f8c1657a5a45218d31c481c2a400e25ac3.svg)\n\n![](images/f4/f4a894860fadbc3ac88fa360cae23ef0f33903f3d930cb00496bb2c2505d2334.png)"] [17592186079683 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $f(x)$ below:\n![](images/e4/e46fcaf79d93513c8b946de51ef451ad8a638936681ce3fc70ffdbe8de023078.svg)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ seems to have a jump discontinuity at $x=-3.$ However, by selecting a particular value for $k$, we can remove the jump discontinuity and make the function continuous.\n\nWe can find this value of $k$ by forcing the left and right-sided limits of the function to be equal at $x=-3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to (-3)^-} f(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x\\to (-3)^+} f(x)\n$$\n\nFor the left limit, we have,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}(3{x}^{2}+kx) \\\\  & =3(-3)^{2}+k(-3) \\\\  & =27-3k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}{x}^{3} \\\\  & =(-3)^{3} \\\\  & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the left and right limits as equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}27-3k & =-27 \\\\ 3k & =54 \\\\ k & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to make $f(x)$ continuous at $x=-3,$ we need to choose $k=18.$"] [17592186079716 :tutorial/content "The additive properties of integrals are beneficial when we have to integrate a piecewise function, such as \n![](images/fa/faef3f4c19469187aa3f4c2beb7a112005a761d2cc100b725b1b507fb34cac95.svg)\n\nFor example, let's calculate the integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-5}^4 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe can split the integral into two integrals using the additive property, one integral for each \"piece\" of the piecewise function.\n\n- The first integral will be over the integral $[-5,0],$ and\n\n- the second integral will be over the integral $[0,4].$\n\nDoing this, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-5}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-5}^{0}x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{4}5\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{|}_{-5}^{0}+5x{|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[{0}^{2}-(-5)^{2}]+5[4-0] \\\\  & =-\\frac{25}{2}+20 \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186079720 :tutorial/content "Let's think about how to graph the solutions to the following two-variable inequality:\n\n![](images/9c/9c366bd1e6e436a908877b903d309078b7a40320a279065ffe795b52eece4a8b.svg) \n\nSince this is a two-variable inequality, we must represent its solutions in the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane. \n\nWe start by replacing the inequality symbol ![](images/79/790374cc61ee8a73065b09d2bc1bfbb37ac255dcac47e7784962c63057025fff.svg) with an equals sign ![](images/03/0390f8b1f8405fa7ec0dd6ce7af3bccd7e0474a8ed59e323595062ced4c42fcf.svg) and plot the resulting line. In this case, we get the line ![](images/09/0959b88bad14750ae0ac71cfc63edbe90a03ae64db7fc0cb56cbf827289f11d5.svg)\n\n![](images/34/3497eb8d3f071794cd899c5c62a88500fbcf7b69a34d58171866a078a185bfc9.png)\n\nNotice that we have a \"*greater than*\" ![](images/79/790374cc61ee8a73065b09d2bc1bfbb37ac255dcac47e7784962c63057025fff.svg) inequality. Therefore, we draw a *dashed* line to denote that points on the line are *not* solutions to our inequality.\n\nNow, since we're solving ![](images/0d/0d93a37b8a2c41f9bfbef6f40271c3a43ae9246dc55b76c6154ddb2a3305f98f.svg) we need to shade all points whose ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-value is *greater than* its ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-value. This is given by all of the points that lie *above* our dashed line. So, let's shade these in.\n\n![](images/3c/3c1df1de40b9ccb8ea9893a0c45ef3072e0380f0a3683d4f7d690e771d0ff852.png)\n\nAnd we're done! All of the points in the shaded region satisfy the inequality ![](images/f5/f55852c049661527d11683dc61ede4846eae70f51ce317220d7fea47cea44823.svg)"] [17592186079721 :tutorial/content "When solving two-variable inequalities, it's often helpful to test to ensure we've shaded the correct region. \n\nTo demonstrate, let's go back to the following inequality:\n\n![](images/9c/9c366bd1e6e436a908877b903d309078b7a40320a279065ffe795b52eece4a8b.svg)\n\nThe solution to this inequality is indicated by the shaded region below.\n\n![](images/cd/cdc0c68029408c7d28f3c57b265de09e172cdaf5f76030f776a08a6842ec47ea.png)\n\nTo check that we've shaded the correct region, we pick a point inside the shaded region and verify that it satisfies the original inequality. Let's pick the point ![](images/fd/fd83db97b0675d3527a9f2a7a68d38a98f2d05ab5b1771d961c0d687e44c191b.svg)\n\n![](images/c2/c28d1722b93fe5a087dfb2675ad468319151d13caa9e9768730bfd9b188698a2.png)\n\nWhen we substitute the coordinates ![](images/42/42f562f132f08838fe095cdeb883c38dd65e0d77828f65f73d783980c52f34ec.svg) into our inequality, we get a true statement:\n\n![](images/cb/cbbf4cbc6d10bb5131aff54087103592cc5719aa699efaf6721287042eb90791.svg) \n\nThis indicates that we have shaded the correct region."] [17592186079722 :tutorial/content "Let's now think about how to graph the solutions to the inequality\n\n![](images/6a/6a25488634d209465bceb496d954cd45546091151404b8b1d4ea4b8959f32a58.svg) \n\nAs before, we start by replacing the inequality symbol ![](images/c1/c1961799d574c1cdf4d3ec008eaa6a13f205bc90c75aa4c5f2b763f2fc7b26d4.svg) with an equals sign ![](images/03/0390f8b1f8405fa7ec0dd6ce7af3bccd7e0474a8ed59e323595062ced4c42fcf.svg) and plot the resulting line ![](images/09/0959b88bad14750ae0ac71cfc63edbe90a03ae64db7fc0cb56cbf827289f11d5.svg)\n\n![](images/72/72b217c75c8ec84681212a8ab48614fb777e05e7022ca338897d16763070c816.png)\n\nNotice that we have a \"*less than*\" ![](images/c1/c1961799d574c1cdf4d3ec008eaa6a13f205bc90c75aa4c5f2b763f2fc7b26d4.svg) inequality. Therefore, we draw a *dashed* line. \n\nNow, since we're solving ![](images/d7/d71368c766686c489e604c80865ad272fbae06736c14dce0d85e4636a9972ed3.svg) we need to shade all points whose ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-value is *smaller than* its ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-value. This is given by all of the points that lie *below* our dashed line.\n\n![](images/7b/7bf436735632fc8e848e3f76577748628cc846ef5186a97011a77c02d1b93347.png)\n\nAnd we're done! All of the points in the shaded region satisfy the inequality ![](images/6a/6a25488634d209465bceb496d954cd45546091151404b8b1d4ea4b8959f32a58.svg)\n\nTo check that we've shaded the correct region, we pick a point inside the shaded region and check that it satisfies the original inequality. Let's pick the point ![](images/ab/ab6b2f25fb90ef8bea8b21d9d0253752c37bb3c77b675547a223b03f97d3bf6a.svg)\n\n![](images/95/95cb11e9786be8ef669469761d9d120693d46a64b6ce4fdb0dd7eb225479b866.png)\n\nWhen we substitute the coordinates ![](images/dc/dcc19bac01833ff23365244490e52b2722741da56e804e9a95b5b264f23216bf.svg) into our inequality, we get a true statement:\n\n![](images/19/198239e0b69706444f4b76f1bd90625df630be1dc0f43f1e35f82ad216e04b29.svg) \n\nThis indicates that we have shaded the correct region."] [17592186082926 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n![](images/4e/4eb97ebf3c509832a78f507427a4dead8caf2e495528121f29caf4f1e6b78882.svg)\n\nWhat are the missing entries in the proof template below?\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n![](images/da/da5c94a138a2a1c37b4eca700822c11d1740bc7f191272bd7b04eb4f0331bf36.svg)\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some ![](images/fb/fb9a89f02c5d1f70a8213f78ab487122536a4488f27a191baddf7cc11e0190ae.svg) we have\n![](images/90/90d5285154002b29f21dc2371e4d8cbe4680e683cc5cef46f0ca8677df1d2510.svg)\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n![](images/05/0531b1f1bc71a31d4cd672c8a78690516d9a68c81870bf1f0723d939e239f4d9.svg)\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ![](images/55/55883265eb6b2ec016ed0eb7afadd4e2d2d5183786e048f032d6d6e19f74a843.svg)\nfor all $n \\in \\mathbb{N}.$"] [17592186082926 :question/worked-solution "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove the following:\n\n![](images/83/833e64bb77220a52ec87ac4c333439ddf6097151aa992508070af2614a51ae36.svg)\n\nWe start by proving the **base case**, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side.\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n![](images/0f/0fd03d4f705ce8e2eb6969f528c847d274acd7ccaf816e2510f331ed8544314c.svg)\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show they're equal.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k \\geq 1.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}k \\geq 1},$ we have\n![](images/79/79ca61162feea82659e68725144768b96b947d21c7d0273f0435b07875b3b400.svg)\n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n![](images/11/11c05d918501f3e52784129b1125fc456d6f61b40f36124ca8666d206fee1c60.svg)\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation using the inductive hypothesis and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we're shown that $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ![](images/df/dff83649ba6f41c29c1578c82159beff07d1355f4ad730dc4485e5af25de8a93.svg)\nfor all $n \\in \\mathbb{N}.$"] [17592186082927 :question/problem "Use mathematical induction to prove that\n![](images/07/07547d9d90fe3939d5ce9a528a2fd37a56980b566d17ee057436b6fcc4694fff.svg)\nfor all $n \\in \\mathbb{N}.$"] [17592186082927 :question/worked-solution "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1), P(2), P(3), \\ldots,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in\\mathbb N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in\\mathbb N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n![](images/d7/d7e3e5ed2dc60dd8a7a40711798ba589649a7a85cfb01630bdc0e63d779eee8e.svg)\n\nWe start by proving the base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that they're equal:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ {4}^{1}-1 & {4}^{1}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\geq 1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\geq 1,$ we have\n![](images/fc/fc4407495fc6621f5e80daf8846c11206289d4be5fe4a8af9f67721291216a89.svg)\n\nWe must show that $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n![](images/7f/7fc2b8a2b2e0ec12f515afcb2d730adf2747ef3cea41f2c97d2d87df9e139118.svg)\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nWhen proving matrix identities using induction, the key to unlocking the proof is to factor out one instance of the matrix, as follows:\n\n![](images/09/099b3ed6a4c4dd958d1df9dbf507c775cf9a4a9f42ed661e5f8937f70cd46109.svg)\n\nLet's apply this technique in this case:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{k+1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ {4}^{k}-1 & {4}^{k}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ {4}^{k}-1+3\\cdot {4}^{k} & 4\\cdot {4}^{k}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4\\cdot {4}^{k}-1 & 4\\cdot {4}^{k}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ {4}^{k+1}-1 & {4}^{k+1}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So let's write down our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction,\n![](images/f6/f67905088dd4d08876f93a46f2997497279c9eaadcdb4cc501ca932cb46265c2.svg)\nis true for all $n\\geq 1.$"] [17592186082934 :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the first equation by ${\\color{blue}{2}}$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n![](images/66/66212282ac1369c4ed9cd55773a11fd792fde2b58e749149531327ef55fa966e.svg)\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\t2x + \\cancel{2y} & = 6\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t6x - \\cancel{2y} & = -2\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\hline\n\t  \t(2x + 6x)  &= (6 - 2)\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t{\\color{red}8x} &= 4\n\t \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${\\color{red}8x}.$"] [17592186082935 :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the second equation by ${\\color{blue}{-3}}$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n![](images/b6/b6daa769fdd6307b6dc1f6e40a42c72cc79a8658362cc4adc9d9730f6607b179.svg)\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together, *eliminate* the variable $x,$ and solve the resulting equation for $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cancel{3x} - 2y & = 9\\\\[5pt]\n-\\cancel{3x} + 9y & = 12\\\\[5pt]\n\\hline\n(9y - 2y) & = (9 + 12)\\\\[5pt]\n{\\color{red}7y} & = 21\\\\[5pt]\ny & = 3\n\t \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y = 3.$ However, we still need to find $x.$ To do this, we substitute $y = 3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3y & =-4 \\\\ x-3(3) & =-4 \\\\ x-9 & =-4 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=5, \\,y = 3,$ which can be expressed as $(5,3).$"] [17592186083004 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the dividend is missing an $x^2$ term, so we insert a $0x^2$ in its place and carry out the process in the same way.\n\n![](images/46/469e53d0e8ca69548d5385e8a4f75c3c366c59ce0c48b7eccf71f6bdb5577fcc.svg)\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ it means that $x+1$ divides $5x^4+x^3+6x+2$ exactly and gives a quotient of $5x^3-4x^2+4x+2.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5x^4+x^3+6x+2}{x+1} = 5x^3-4x^2+4x+2 .\n$$"] [17592186083005 :question/worked-solution "First, we write the divisor in standard form as $-3x+2.$ Then, we factor out the leading coefficient. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{4}+7{x}^{3}-10x+8}{-3x+2} & =\\frac{3{x}^{4}+7{x}^{3}-10x+8}{-3(x-\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\frac{3{x}^{4}+7{x}^{3}-10x+8}{(x-\\frac{2}{3})}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCarrying out the synthetic division process on $\\dfrac{3x^4+7x^3-10x+8}{\\left(x-\\frac23\\right)},$ remembering to include a coefficient of zero for the $x^2$ term, we arrive at the following:\n\n![](images/7a/7a44598088833fa475191b39579bb82935074ae5c470e5a2139555818b91af08.svg)\n\nReading off the bottom row, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x^4+7x^3-10x+8}{\\left(x-\\frac23\\right)} = 3x^3+9x^2+6x-6+\\frac{4}{\\left(x-\\frac23\\right)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply by the original $-\\dfrac 1 3$ factor to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{4}+7{x}^{3}-10x+8}{2-3x} & =-\\frac{1}{3}(3{x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}+6x-6+\\frac{4}{(x-\\frac{2}{3})}) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}-2x+2+\\frac{4}{2-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186083022 :question/worked-solution "The number of permutations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n_{n}P_r = \\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $20$ employees, so $n=20.$ We want to select $4$ of those employees to fill the roles of president, vice president, treasurer, and secretary, so we have $r=4.$\n\nTherefore, the number of different ways to fill the roles is\n\n![](images/1a/1ae525b0e04502c3c1f4c8eb4ffd01419c48cbde67cbd721ad58300dc2f0ebb1.svg)"] [17592186083080 :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola \n![](images/e1/e1b79fe61429e51171e07ceda9eefc6dcf727e59e7f6eb820fe64a0fe3041553.svg)"] [17592186083080 :question/worked-solution "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at ![](images/3a/3a6333dccc007be0992f95974a58ce3d652e5ecafcaaa865a24ff8b42bcfc37a.svg) is given by\n![](images/68/68085f6e83d4aba3b05bd631d523432bb0e664ceac36b8c707102ead6e0f9460.svg)\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in the above form, we need to group ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) and ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg) terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n![](images/f5/f5250f1f7f213970a934f87b2bf9a59417348deaa854492e6c40d6a3c9ab8dc3.svg)\n\nTherefore, the center is ![](images/51/51c3c69238e5ffe9c22e9a9a50dd89f69899db2c0895c5f39e2d80db74c6b3db.svg)"] [17592186083112 :question/problem "Evaluate ![](images/77/777f12c125eef00b30f90e43ba94af90ac4b02b71c805da5b18e3306d03fba7d.svg)"] [17592186083119 :question/problem "In box ![](images/94/94276c7f8de725803347541a3a00e124915f54c89e0908f256f903f32e990c73.png) there are ![](images/26/26eecbf5965af5ad8a5a3ef715bb6e0fa969d36ffafbccaccea2a19616b25eb2.png) marbles, ![](images/a1/a1aa710a9396fec0487bfd728e836f7188caa9a03b1d66d79d88948ba32e32ca.png) of which are green. In box ![](images/76/762b6cc7cc4d4c8fc3553d61e1779ae5e08597476c0101011b9d3824730e88ed.png) there are ![](images/cc/cc635e1b92c71b63fbb85b82a6e5057ccec707d58b8a3a8c64d558004d1a26d1.png) marbles, ![](images/4f/4f94b41c8502c080528c0ca826a6a02aa2e60acba9b8506c41cfb9629e2a2077.png) of which are green. The marbles from the two boxes are mixed, and one marble is randomly chosen from the mixture. What is the probability that the selected marble is green?"] [17592186083119 :question/worked-solution "Let ![](images/51/512ba11ac54abfbb3af0e93bc113799e39e8d81b0925bdf9ddd773b792a9284f.png) be the event that the chosen marble is green, and let ![](images/33/33afe640f4fdb603b7dfb684ccbdaade53c295b1d26443b458712e4bb55985bf.png) be the event that the chosen marble came from box ![](images/08/08bd02340c9c39d2dd0fa163a1f89978e6959c962385733e3f60f82e9e3ccdb8.png) Then ![](images/b4/b43ce7cccfeb3161ca716d624c07927988c87c8b12ebe97974d1901544b7e7ad.png) is the event that the chosen marble came from box ![](images/75/756cc5b7560fd9f0a8deecfe5d1f649978b121e1490065242f2a8d0d9a88e122.png)\n\nTo calculate the probability that the randomly selected marble is green, we can use the law of total probability:\n![](images/2e/2e831059f27fc478d0e9074c858d317f3a34f40219c0b0b3d98f6f3d67edb74e.png)\n\nTo calculate ![](images/4f/4fb2133dd2933224a6f0d891be21ecbe068a922eaf1f5c17cbf5f370a7ae675b.png) we take the number of green marbles that came from box ![](images/de/de28878467ff508d37108258a8d0f7985f8898da6e238e98aad8f53f7b2abd49.png) ![](images/79/79ed9e30b8d0ce49876979c9ad02d4e6a32c2b3db73cf7c7ebf605c35e95460f.png) and divide by the total number of marbles in the mixture ![](images/20/2047d3b4bda0c588c7c26a6dd8e9b43b64b5e126cdc155282ee3d296a15554ec.png) So,\n\n![](images/16/16f0eedeee65a71dd9d8fa83535d5f65bff44f1c424663fb2b393046c131e696.png)\n\nSimilarly, to calculate ![](images/9b/9b1bb30112995dd5d0f3e6cd147c26736878279484f82d4d73a086b8efdaec9a.png) we take the number of green marbles that came from box ![](images/27/27b99f04c6f0f2b24f2d107248a2315f6b2d2574f849bf47379d8f2fdd27d05e.png) ![](images/b7/b758dc5e36b9ce2daba82e94d58b5232cecff327cf85f3a2571675c5845ba90d.png) and divide by the total number of marbles in the mixture ![](images/75/75c92641ef9ece42d3dab493a8e8fd78d276cd4f40e423f2b442d23464fb47c7.png) So,\n\n![](images/6d/6ddd96143aa9754eb6b266d82b854a41fcd3e17d9d9c5aeec7d44a669e1994b3.png)\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected marble is green is\n![](images/a1/a1fb9fe4e3ce980bd3385ece84df7522afee0f98b5e57cf82e8c7e0a19a4119c.png)"] [17592186083123 :question/worked-solution "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n![](images/e8/e8a1e7972e4e4a543c2e774894f83ec38755e7b2145a5b301e44906affac9c15.svg)\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-1,4],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/5c/5ca4ceb0bed3882328dbfa8fb54eb9de948a53d66d9850b8d40781d19f50f4fc.svg)\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0<X<3) & =P(0<X<3) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3-0}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186083129 :question/worked-solution "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n![](images/e7/e76831e0e537ad6fdd429115b56034f54f9071574430c41fb9fe5b4888a72b48.svg)\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $\\left[0, 10\\right],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/7a/7a7bfa98d0a54a198c0050e763a2f1ac75bca751063b9ea20ec6979163049c6f.svg)\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 7) & =P(0\\le X\\le 7) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{7}\\frac{1}{10}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{7-0}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186083130 :question/worked-solution "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $Y$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n![](images/64/64661e76a430c63a3ba74d43f3d074e43d5c03a1a09c39d714a087d5fb8b5afa.svg)\n\nOur random variable $Y$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $\\left[1, 16\\right],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/bd/bd2bfc727ccdaeb4d0912640c12fa99900db52102aefe3a3dce46ad2e2cbfef0.svg)\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 10) & =P(10\\le Y\\le 16) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{10}^{16}f(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{10}^{16}\\frac{1}{15}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{16-10}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186084397 :tutorial/content "A repeating decimal can be expressed as a geometric series whose common ratio is a power of $\\dfrac 1 {10}.$\n\nFor example, the number $0.\\overline{8}$ can be expressed as \n![](images/03/03acd5b86ea1c8f214f08482c17a8631bd8d55754d4e5ddd974fa17a9ac9cb02.svg)\nWe have $a_1 = \\dfrac{8}{10}$ and $r = \\dfrac {0.08}{0.8} = \\dfrac 1 {10}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.\\overset{―}{8} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{10}{\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)}^{n}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)}^{n}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)}^{n}\\cdot 10 \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186084439 :tutorial/content "Our goal is to find the quotient and remainder of the following division problem.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^3-5x^2-7x+11}{x-3}\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can use **synthetic division**. Synthetic division allows us to divide a polynomial by a factor of the form $(x-c).$ Here's how we do it.\n\n| ![](images/86/8657b84c4cee9d122c5ec7fb62e652133c02b433e9bb73fb690573dd29857fe6.svg) | Start off by writing the term $c$ of the divisor on the left side. Here, $c = {\\color{blue}{3}}.$ Then, write the coefficients of all terms of the dividend on the right side in descending order of their degree. Remember the sign! Also, if a term is missing, use $0$ as the coefficient. |\n| --- | --- |\n| ![](images/89/89ff1cd5f573ae387a716a8a446cd0867dfd464df0d9d9d91d4e9fd1f17f31ca.svg) | Bring down the leading term of the dividend. Here, the leading term is ${\\color{red}2}.$ |\n| ![](images/80/80e8e2f6092444058ec459f28518d0bd66b47858b9605562f8041aa09acda45e.svg) | Multiply the leading term $\\color{red}2$ by ${\\color{blue}3 }.$ This gives ${{\\color{blue}{3}}\\cdot \\color{red}{2}} = {\\color{green}6}.$ Place the result below the next term. |\n| ![](images/a4/a44903e5fb107c1737c9ca1a6d0ba869784c7443b81068591bdb4ac0f7489555.svg) | Add the last result ($\\color{green}{6}$) to the term located above ($\\color{magenta}{-5}$) and write their sum below the line. This gives ${\\color{magenta}{-5}} + {\\color{green}{6}}={\\color{red}{1}}.$ |\n| ![](images/a6/a62d39bda1c2e3463ec07179055c47ac775369e9c0065725b9b607f70f56b68b.svg) | We now repeat the process. Multiply the last result ($\\color{red}1$ here) by $\\color{blue} 3$ and place the result below the next term. This gives ${\\color{blue}{3}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{1}}= {\\color{green}{3}}.$ |\n| ![](images/73/73578072793a196046848a135a745d97d17a711987919e6cc8bb96cf9c9712e0.svg) | Now add the last result ($\\color{green}{3}$) to the term located above ($\\color{magenta}{-7}$) and write their sum below the line. This gives ${\\color{magenta}{-7}} + {\\color{green}{3}}={\\color{red}{-4}}.$ |\n| ![](images/7f/7fc91ddc948d8471c21fc38f7a4aca73204ca7ded9f5e867ae1dd2aa2dfde1f3.svg) | There is one last step. Again, multiply $\\color{blue} 3$ by the last result $\\color{red}-4$ and write it below the constant term. This gives ${\\color{blue}{3}}\\cdot{\\color{red}{(-4)}}={\\color{green}-12}.$ Finally, add $\\color{green}-12$ to $\\color{magenta}11$ to get ${\\color{blue}{-1}},$ which is the remainder. The remainder $\\color{blue}-1$ is important, so we put a box around it. |\n\nThe numbers $2,$ $1$ and $-4$ in the bottom row are the coefficients of the **quotient**. So from left to right, the quotient is\n\n$$\n2x^2+1x-4.\n$$\n\nThe **remainder** is the value in the box, so ${\\color{blue}-1}$ in this case. \n\nFinally, we can write the full result of the division as \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^3-5x^2-7x+11}{x-3}=2x^2+x-4+\\dfrac{\\color{blue}-1}{x-3} ,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^3-5x^2-7x+11}{x-3}=2x^2+x-4-\\dfrac{1}{x-3}.\n$$"] [17592186084450 :tutorial/content "In general, the number of unique combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_nC_r = \\frac{{}_nP_r}{r!} = \\dfrac{n!}{(n-r)!r!}.\n$$\n\nThis is pronounced \"en-see-arr.\" The \"C\" stands for \"combination.\" \n\nIn the previous example, we had $n=5$ books, taking $r=3$ books with us on the trip. Using the formula, the total number of combinations is computed as\n![](images/e7/e73f73afd887785d06875907181fa38097aac62a65a81f5da464b674763ff863.svg)\n\nWe got the same result as before!\n\n**Note:** An alternative notation for ${}_nC_r$ is $\\displaystyle {{n}\\choose{r}}.$"] [17592186084456 :tutorial/content "The **rational roots theorem** (or **rational zeros theorem**) describes the rational roots of a polynomial with integer coefficients. The statement of the theorem is as follows:\n\n*Suppose that the rational number $\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}{p}}}{\\color{red}{q}},$ in lowest terms, is a root of a polynomial with integer coefficients. Then $\\color{blue}p$ must be a factor of the polynomial's constant term, and $\\color{red}q$ must be a factor of its leading coefficient.*\n\nIn other words, given a polynomial with integer coefficients, any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\dfrac{\\textrm{factor of the constant term}}{\\textrm{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(x) = {\\color{red}{8}}x^3+12x^2+6x+{\\color{blue}{2}}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the rational roots theorem, any rational root of $f(x)$ must have a numerator that is a factor of $\\color{blue}{2}$ (that is, either $\\color{blue} \\pm1$ or $\\color{blue} \\pm2$) and a denominator that's a factor of $\\color{red} 8$ (${\\color{red} \\pm1},$ ${\\color{red} \\pm2},$ ${\\color{red} \\pm4},$ or $\\color{red} \\pm8$). \n\nAt first glance, it may seem like we have $16$ possible rational roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{8}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHowever, if we reduce the above fractions to lowest form, we see that there are some duplicates, which we cancel out:\n\n![](images/91/91d3a123202f03ecec2d568548b5964fdc941e77d9edbad8ab386954bb046740.svg) \n\nRemoving the duplicates, we have ten *possible* rational roots. We list them from least to greatest in absolute value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm 1, & \\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Watch out!** The above roots are only *possible* rational roots. They are *not* guaranteed to be roots of our polynomial $f(x).$ The only guarantee is that if $f(x)$ has any roots that are rational numbers, they are included in the list of numbers above. Here, it turns out that the only actual root among the possibilities is $-1.$"] [17592186084471 :tutorial/content "In general, the number of permutations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n_{n}P_r = \\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}\n$$\n\nIn the previous example, we had $n=5$ books and $r=3$ slots in the bookshelf. Using the formula, the total number of permutations is computed as\n![](images/3d/3d53f73ea9e1a5d0966237c002f2d11075ee10d519bb6a2823b2ff0fea8c8e53.svg)\n\nWe got the same result as before!\n\n**Note:** We can write ${}_nP_r$ in a number of ways:\n\n$$\n{}_nP_r = {}^nP_r = P(n,r)\n$$\n\nAll of these mean the same thing."] [17592186084476 :tutorial/content "Suppose ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) and ![](images/75/7533ad047439b7d799dd7afee5d6a7bc9f4404fbadb8c3f27ef6d3952119ff6b.svg) are two events in a random experiment.\nThe conditional probability ![](images/57/576f25eda2a4f4b8004bee0945ba88362617547b6498cbdc200cb7114a38e0d7.svg) is the probability that the event ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) occurs *given that ![](images/75/7533ad047439b7d799dd7afee5d6a7bc9f4404fbadb8c3f27ef6d3952119ff6b.svg) has already occurred*.\n\nWe've already seen how to compute conditional probabilities using Venn diagrams. But is there a formula that we can use?\n\nBy definition, we say that ![](images/57/576f25eda2a4f4b8004bee0945ba88362617547b6498cbdc200cb7114a38e0d7.svg) is given by the formula\n\n![](images/ad/adaa2c430122663f1249a422ae60292ca1c826971dca0a46798fbb66eb9561bd.svg)\n\nFor instance, let's suppose we have different shaped and colored candies in a bag, and we take one out at random. Let's assume that the probability of picking a green circle candy is ![](images/3a/3a51254ae79153d6f03412adbc61ab4b9289d090a78eee93d6fdf7b095bfb624.svg) while the probability of picking a green candy is ![](images/c6/c6f3ee2917057e25cb4eb459b0bcfbeacc530d386dc2c1c9839a638063cbf801.svg) Then, what is the probability that the candy we have picked is a circle, given that it is green?\n\nLet's call ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) the event \"the candy is a circle\" and ![](images/75/7533ad047439b7d799dd7afee5d6a7bc9f4404fbadb8c3f27ef6d3952119ff6b.svg) the event \"the candy is green.\" Then we need to find ![](images/b0/b0e6dab10f3c6515e3378df3672a0752213e3e0a65c68c89c0443fca49473e61.svg)\n\n- The probability of picking a green circle candy is ![](images/3a/3a51254ae79153d6f03412adbc61ab4b9289d090a78eee93d6fdf7b095bfb624.svg) so ![](images/58/58570601156b0b580ad6018902eea748ad371578c948962b6060faa28c62af67.svg)\n\n- The probability of picking a green candy is ![](images/19/19396f6c2d19827db32af5f2d5b135318e08e56751991bcb7f797e96382eca81.svg) therefore, ![](images/6a/6a7202efcd726bbea2aa6f74ff3b7b5f8f20feeffa98cc49a08dde18c26ffe8c.svg)\n\nSo, using the definition of a conditional probability, we get\n\n![](images/18/18b1fbf925e7539ebed923bfc567a0e24de0b6d04eba62d9e6cee5f8a4a26e40.svg)"] [17592186084481 :tutorial/content "A continuous random variable $X$ follows a **continuous uniform distribution** on the interval $[a,b]$ if all values of $X$ are equally likely on $[a,b].$\n\nIntuitively, the familiar statement \"choose a random number between $a$ and $b$\" can be translated as \"observe a value from the continuous uniform distribution on $[a,b].$\" \n\nThe probability density function for the continuous uniform distribution on an interval $[a,b]$ takes the following form:\n\n![](images/a2/a247cacd484a99d579d2a30245d7ab495f6d0d116fbf02e2fe8a95fa658df9c6.svg)\n\nThis corresponds to the following graph:\n\n![](images/9d/9d077084b025c673533cefc52b7d2e7bfe90e8f903e926a27ce8d1207a0d4e79.png)\n\nNotice that the probability density of the continuous uniform distribution on $[a,b]$ is just $1$ divided by the length of the interval. This ensures that our probability density function has the same value everywhere in the interval and integrates to $1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{1}{b-a}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\frac{x}{b-a}|}_{a}^{b} \\\\  & =\\frac{b-a}{b-a} \\\\  & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen a random variable $X$ follows a continuous uniform distribution on the interval $[a,b],$ we often write\n\n$$\nX\\sim U[a,b]\n$$\n\n \n\nand say that \"$X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[a,b].$\""] [17592186084484 :tutorial/content "The **lowest common multiple** (or $\\textrm{LCM}$) of two integers is the smallest multiple that's common to both numbers.\n\nTo find the lowest common multiple of two small numbers, we can list all multiples of the two numbers and identify the smallest multiple that they have in common.\n\nFor example, let's find the lowest common multiple of $4$ and $6.$ We start by listing all multiples of $4$ and $6\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & \\,\\to \\,4,\\,8,\\,\\boxed{\\text{[math]12[/math]}},\\,16,\\,20,\\,24,\\,… \\\\ 6 & \\,\\to \\,6,\\,\\boxed{\\text{[math]12[/math]}},\\,18,\\,24,\\,30,\\,36,\\,…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe smallest multiple in both lists is ![](images/92/9285d37c7b455395acb42a54f78348bedfd8c9a72ad63d6e6ab7c2426148ea0d.svg) So $12$ is our answer, and we can write\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(4,6)= 12.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The lowest common multiple is also sometimes called the *least* common multiple."] [17592186084489 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to solve the following rational equation. \n\n![](images/c5/c5b84db354c24a0b6f4c45ecc857668a6514c51161867b6797a37acea25d0bd1.svg)\n\nNotice that each of the denominators in our equation is either a binomial or product of binomials. \n\nFrom here, we make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator (![](images/22/22a645a9e1c8260201c66c6af84cdf1c9d06b534716a2e31be23d8f6e744f5cb.svg)) is the least common multiple (![](images/2d/2db8412ff4edf9f090c02dda4c50cd1562b84f6ae99cb74f75750881a3ca2aa3.svg)) of the denominators, which in this case is given by\n![](images/47/47cf83f34dba1af580d8d103f4b744888633f83657f127b148a717f7bba7b392.svg)\n\n- The values ![](images/4a/4ac5ad4e585fa76e28a628e40b8284f2cf3127d51053b40815b9d06cd5e28afa.svg) and ![](images/d4/d463819d45d8f511fb456fbbcf014015220fb127acab0d427d1954e5674f4620.svg) cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n![](images/7b/7b8b7376a449d72d6560bc2ee8f29836c346e35ddd8b282468aa8190c852e3c0.svg)\n\nAfter canceling, we are left with the equation\n\n![](images/36/367bb9f537cc6d99f4d5a92953119e2fd57d23a9851ab0a7d32af68902314da3.svg)\n\nSolving this equation for ![](images/f6/f6ea97a79535415eaed351835491b6719dba8181044eed7f94e41feb7bcac8d6.svg) we get the following:\n\n![](images/27/27ff1b0ed70f265f4c39f32116b2d019cbb514c8f27a43fbd7e8e8e1fb4d2ffa.svg)\n\nSo, ![](images/60/600957eb8a00416e73e77c1866e0c065b489f6d32faa3215f3cef167ad8fcc21.svg) is a *potential* solution. \n\nWe now need to check this value against the prohibited solutions ![](images/4a/4ac5ad4e585fa76e28a628e40b8284f2cf3127d51053b40815b9d06cd5e28afa.svg) and ![](images/d4/d463819d45d8f511fb456fbbcf014015220fb127acab0d427d1954e5674f4620.svg) mentioned earlier. Now, since ![](images/94/948de5597ce1e4ad93b6d3344b463694d2cf1780903ca30a916a784d672e4d7c.svg) and ![](images/fa/fa8945e1ccf216ac0b94b3ae625f4c284190630782a4c0c2e24f4a94ea520d40.svg) we have that ![](images/60/600957eb8a00416e73e77c1866e0c065b489f6d32faa3215f3cef167ad8fcc21.svg) is indeed a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the solution to our original equation is ![](images/09/0981bec86ce7424eb7dabbaa3827cc7591360dc24997730f3773c002f72a673b.svg)"] [17592186084500 :tutorial/content "Recall that the ![](images/c5/c594a328984a211291c3547317baeb153ed97e2db7f43ab25ce204c31fe1796c.svg) \"reverses\" the action of ![](images/bc/bc562d54615dad55d095db2afed7626f62ff7ebf0ab6c5d45471b77ac30b6398.svg) so that\n![](images/ea/eab0458598a0b4298ccbfcd1664babf085309c0017ba0e530c76900f38bb8678.svg)\nwhich can be visualized using the mapping diagram, as shown below.\n\n![](images/3d/3dd33f3f90eaadec2f1168db89665d1986e562005fc01cd9f58dbc3f17e7a616.png)\n\n \n\nSince ![](images/aa/aabc5ff96d4807ff058c8f3e9f78aa70ced5e624ac2ae1ebdd1bb4597d6c6dad.svg) is just the composition ![](images/5a/5aeac36928f2e7a97b7c71c2c97dd9bef3774970b3efc871c9131c6d2075be5e.svg) we can now give the algebraic definition of the inverse. \n\nThe function ![](images/c5/c594a328984a211291c3547317baeb153ed97e2db7f43ab25ce204c31fe1796c.svg) is called the **inverse of the function ![](images/bc/bc562d54615dad55d095db2afed7626f62ff7ebf0ab6c5d45471b77ac30b6398.svg)** if\n![](images/52/52a4e32d6165988fe50c0766a1310fd3afb895f4a11149ff7b45c1cb0dd76e3a.svg)\nfor each ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) in the domain of ![](images/a0/a047a10a3ed3f5e86676e8fca21093abfe6a1849d90ce94b62101f34fca37e3d.svg)\n\nIn other words, to show that two functions ![](images/93/93f888a1ee0b7da5e29f442f803f1c0eaf73ad02f5eaa97e85bb8a35d607015b.svg) and ![](images/a9/a9e111e418e432fd154a819bb521c373ca64e9b27bbeed17a5777bba6940c6b6.svg) are inverses of each other, we need to check the following condition:\n![](images/7f/7facf5a55f1e90f9b9aac2358e4bc4b0560c2bd4b9fa2700e902a4dde84f90b7.svg)"] [17592186084515 :tutorial/content "Now, suppose that we want to solve the following system of linear equations using elimination:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y &=1 \\\\ x-y &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAs it stands, adding the two equations will not eliminate any of the variables. \n\nHowever, suppose we want to eliminate the variable $x$. We begin by multiplying the second equation by $\\color{blue}-1$, which gives the following equivalent system:\n\n![](images/85/85996afab1889f8032a295f4db6bbbcbc8670a0bc12f0b64f53a3b809f111628.svg)\n\nThen, we add the two resulting equations together. This eliminates the variable $x,$ making it possible to solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{x}+2y & =1 \\\\ -\\cancel{x}+y & =2 \\\\ (2y+y) & =(1+2) \\\\ 3y & =3 \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y=1.$ However, we still need to find $x.$ To do this, we substitute $y=1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x-y & =-2 \\\\ x-(1) & =-2 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-1$ and $y=1,$ which can be expressed as $(-1, 1).$"] [17592186084520 :tutorial/content "Let's prove the following result using mathematical induction:\n\n![](images/f9/f9106bf23db3c56425757ea9b8b3aef7adf5cbe926212746c85c63e4193f308d.svg) for all $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n![](images/82/82fb358f5e84e37e035da6cfa101c65f6e43b9bad75c63299a56999b125c7f63.svg)\n\nWe start by proving the base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that they're equal:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2(1) \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\geq 1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\geq 1,$ we have\n![](images/7e/7e1416be5b4270fdfe3051c74f1db5106556ed860c9f88fcbdd866f48ff3339b.svg)\n\nWe must show that $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n![](images/e0/e0042401a1070f9f1977a7005f6875f8bc14886842a908fcc78f33678fdcb39b.svg)\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nWhen proving matrix identities using induction, the key to unlocking the proof is to factor out one instance of the matrix, as follows: \n\n![](images/06/06dc062c2c15fba27384ac9ec89ddbe2827ea1901ed74ccef1f97dcf11991d54.svg)\n\nLet's apply this technique in this case:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k+1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2k \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2+2k \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2(k+1) \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction,\n![](images/2a/2a3c4d7370e4036aea2b0ad07bdee4a244ea828bef025178c8919be08a410b23.svg)\nis true for all $n \\in \\mathbb{N}.$"] [17592186084526 :tutorial/content "Let's plot the graph of ![](images/e3/e31f27c483c52d7933201e37d1f6020c20d7903402be16f28ffd407c7f3db93a.svg). According to the algebraic definition of absolute value, we have\n![](images/eb/ebc6cc8f3964043ffa2ff15f7e3501222794753f30577e4b71037efb2e4ffa30.svg)\nSo, the left-hand branch of the graph is the function ![](images/8f/8f96aa73ce843c7815a1fed6bf3f3774a173cfa4d56f13e1f3a8c48d53cd7097.svg), and the right-hand branch is ![](images/23/238e977be23f692486c8bf68f6b40255435b3d1796cc50d260a014a8c670be7b.svg):\n\n![](images/60/60ad4dfdb6086f20f5137c5b4ac4905e55d5697ec4cf31575bf79be5fd408e0c.png)\n\nNotice that the graph has a sharp corner at ![](images/c1/c1e8c20213b45135f50bdc8d4544220f7b0f943ff3ca49439660c86d017e789f.svg) We call this point the **vertex** or the **minimum point.**\n\nWe can apply **horizontal** and **vertical translations** to the graph of ![](images/e3/e31f27c483c52d7933201e37d1f6020c20d7903402be16f28ffd407c7f3db93a.svg).\n\n- The graph of ![](images/91/91c9b5c48d46f81336e889febab90a9027a96accef8626bd0743ca81042f25b8.svg) is a **vertical translation** of the absolute value graph ![](images/0c/0cb4d1ae0c2e2451edd095b9dbcca94222e8ef0514db46745a2acce4b617d78e.svg) It moves the graph ![](images/34/349015631ec44ef653936c85586d2c5ef56ba64392b3f92b4b92cbef3e731bc5.svg) units up.\n\n![](images/cd/cd8b826034f7c3668cacb490e0fab60daaf7c5cae63975bfa5cae9d468c7dec6.png)\n\n- The graph of ![](images/30/3016548be8be38ace3450d51a10371543820a3357b1adbf809d69c23c70f14bb.svg) is a **horizontal translation** of the absolute value graph ![](images/0c/0cb4d1ae0c2e2451edd095b9dbcca94222e8ef0514db46745a2acce4b617d78e.svg) It moves the graph ![](images/34/349015631ec44ef653936c85586d2c5ef56ba64392b3f92b4b92cbef3e731bc5.svg) units to the *right*.\n\n![](images/65/65912d2831dd525964b78a20f2a707d7d828daa2a5e2ebd0793c6b337a42e360.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When drawing horizontal and vertical translations, it is easiest to transform the graph's vertex first. Then, the two branches can be drawn coming out of the vertex.\n\n- When we translate the graph ![](images/87/87f4b5227cb69bd468d75d826fe448c91ae677181f9142fcbe9bc757d0acea77.svg) the resulting curve is called the **image** of ![](images/e3/e31f27c483c52d7933201e37d1f6020c20d7903402be16f28ffd407c7f3db93a.svg) under the translation."] [17592186087457 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n![](images/12/12aec388c94c8c4d4f2099b98f5a489d110fa3ef0d58e7895d9a425e49f8eb77.png)"] [17592186087457 :question/worked-solution "We note that this equation is a homogeneous first-order differential equation because\n![](images/c3/c3215663fd1a332f750b8af4049404061e2b1c64726c81572d1a1e76816bb718.png)\n\nThe given equation is not separable, but we can transform it into a separable equation by carrying out the substitution\n![](images/7c/7c57d5a7c4003269ba9eacf6240394f508347989a9d75665c7070e31cbdad701.png)\n\nCarrying out the substitution and separating variables, we have\n![](images/8a/8ad7a31876bc67fa69ff24ed41badcf6531ddf52b2d1847af4b0b75595ab5bcd.png)\n\nIntegrating both sides, we get\n![](images/7a/7a6577d6ac4f3f1fc100a2767d77509fbe211f7d3e7bc7a2bba027080e7e6ccd.png)\nwhere ![](images/42/4247e7a16afb46979bc6ecfc9ae10dc6c92d0f5f1ff5e1b2a1f412ec86a48a83.png) (with ![](images/9e/9e71f72815a80396d1186bfed1047662e1d507561c7160d1c2d89c2f23421c09.png)) and ![](images/01/012dd077bd0f68f82ae37fa50d493e97bd67ac5ff9f301e48ca29e91b1af16ab.png) \n\nFinally, substituting ![](images/f7/f74fcd854fc4be51cd8eed8f4d0649bcc7cf11089a7aa2a7e7edd8d306693f78.png) and simplifying, we get\n![](images/05/05eae130ba10a3386a1c8621ecc3e3d3ec0bb7af352fb4c928cf8bf2d88c21a3.png)\nwhere ![](images/89/8939a3ee49356a0cc8515cce98d84ecf10279dad9d2f76fac07660d0f99a0d7b.png) Therefore, the general solution is\n![](images/18/185e5a72b055183d70fb6d9dbc0227190a8114b80b2dc4193d8f542d5c85c0d7.png)"] [17592186087540 :question/problem "Write the complex number ![](images/af/af471b7c6676fbc559c8ad2df178f0837e6a56d05b2f50580ebccc25b623f3c3.png), shown in the following Argand diagram, in exponential form.\n\n![](images/33/335311049419928acc946721242c6e8e4096313dca1290b89b1cc7a2c30748fd.png)"] [17592186087540 :question/worked-solution "The complex number shown in the Argand diagram can be written as\n\n![](images/81/8122d85593d59b415fec35244193bbafc13ae12f8ff4dde48ca02281601d900a.png)\n\nTo write it in the form ![](images/3f/3f2c3d3dae4f9753b6d10f23a54a5c696563f987eed9db46fdc0e98240be525f.png) where ![](images/b0/b072dc2e57a8c391baaa173a8c3aa83609632153dffcfc428275cb3605903dc1.png) and ![](images/3d/3d99ff2a6f74a02139c03d8b68342ebca232dfaf4c8f45ffade75f019c46d7d3.png) we first calculate ![](images/06/06f1b516e0b39f038159895642434162b17fac697cb26d958a55e6cb8ee62ce2.png)\n![](images/9e/9e5c3422b05de9a2d8ae88ca77bb87f4fda21745193264842f91ceb7b60f3dde.png)\n\nNow, since ![](images/af/af471b7c6676fbc559c8ad2df178f0837e6a56d05b2f50580ebccc25b623f3c3.png) lies in the second quadrant, we can find the angle ![](images/59/59a1b9eac7eae7e2da1897fb24e0ea23d4e41a3867ed06b8e0d20d07f0db48e8.png) as\n![](images/de/de34ceef6ea8076d6649dfb7c9a50c1963e87ce39b7d8f4fb61bea285e583df7.png)\n\nto three decimal places.\n\nFinally, substituting ![](images/1c/1c0bebef993564be6aa4f68a3902c2e8a38b77710ec1813cf288eaaf01363b38.png) and ![](images/e1/e139f1504c53b16e6d12ff56d39491e4920d4b33837c1f655bb62e4dc914005b.png) into ![](images/cf/cf6438ee2ebf83496eea2855c5e61b9c8133fbcdf194880a772682c4e06fccea.png) we get \n![](images/8b/8b915d7acce2a64610d8c4da76f4472a66b3931f6ee19be63a3828f64c4eb2fe.png)"] [17592186087566 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n![](images/10/10c3819b661a1831f747c949089a9133b4d0df7b07a66efe588c12bab469136e.png)\n\nThe triangle $\\triangle ABC$ is shown below. Find $\\triangle A'B'C',$ the image of $\\triangle ABC$ under the action of $\\mathbf T.$\n\n![](images/49/4986bd2dab6b455c0d5e41909377c433d761bf7270364d3e2596e6db7f23981b.png)"] [17592186087566 :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $T$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $\\triangle ABC\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/0c/0cd1d198e714f41437302bdf21c17979409a533d3055b5b4dc519855d6fc8efb.png)\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $\\triangle A'B'C'$ are $(-3,-4), (0,0), (1,-2),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/e8/e8a14ffbaab4597a2a4a7c9a6bad61dabbc4970fc22c3ed2eb28b2594a670938.png)"] [17592186087567 :question/problem "What linear transformation is represented by the matrix\n![](images/03/0394b5d3a371caf95d5e701c0ffb00894eb685e706d8b70af2149057de0b0cb5.png)"] [17592186087568 :question/problem "A linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a dilation. Given that ![](images/bd/bd6574ee05b60919de656a889f8532e5a512b381eae63f7a65f251a7afe72738.png) and ![](images/be/be73c2a933a6fb99eaa389774a618848bdadd9cafdf0fcd2f6af126b49b87707.png) what is the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}?$"] [17592186087568 :question/worked-solution "We can write $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v) & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}24 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we have $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}) = \\dfrac{1}{4}\\mathbf{v},$ we conclude that the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ is a dilation with scale factor $k = \\dfrac{1}{4}.$\n\nRemember that the matrix representing a dilation of scale factor $k$ is given by\n![](images/48/4866597a50fbdb530e9275c77f2ca926c932c7cc334635bd05191aaddd510d75.png)\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n![](images/96/962b9458714f9b7f6b17fe2259e140dfabba443d8ecb5b9e2328172ab3f8a1a2.png)"] [17592186087569 :question/problem "![](images/8d/8d5b37b3da99f0b93625ab8fd6ea72615da3159a8360fe267c3ae4a470fdd7af.svg)\nConsider the standard matrix $T$ of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^2 \\to \\mathbb{R}^3$ shown above. What is the value of $a-b?$"] [17592186087569 :question/worked-solution "Given the vector ![](images/01/01841df44c0029de31dd06a446211abb0bc0f6852d7bc76797204ae1d9a71e6c.svg) we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -5 \\\\ -3 & -6 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8x-5y \\\\ -3x-6y \\\\ -4x+5y\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5y-8x \\\\ -3x-6y \\\\ 5y-4x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ is given by\n![](images/74/74c14f8d5615c146713a3c8ed31368a9843ed06f91d88a8745adf41764f8a003.svg)\n\nFinally, $a-b=(-5)-(-4)=-1.$"] [17592186087570 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $T$ and the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ given below. If $T$ is the standard matrix of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^3 \\to \\mathbb{R}^2,$ find $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}).$\n\n![](images/46/46bb3e9777a3944eb6b2f458911571442f57a5fae2f7aed1bb924522c51cad4a.svg)"] [17592186087584 :question/problem "Find the standard matrix that  represents  the  linear  transformation $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^3 \\to \\mathbb{R}^2,$ where\n![](images/fa/facc0c74a881851fc58355c5bf85ebb1e073d543a6c5e708e025ebbd61c69043.svg)"] [17592186087584 :question/worked-solution "We require a $2\\times 3$ matrix ${T}$ such that\n![](images/98/9839b4e4af6ba3ee215318c0a9b69844f33c0eea9bf426eb11701e60f3be91b3.svg)\n\nNotice that\n![](images/26/2697e4b0883667c64a7f1c24c370c6fb1f2e0b02c1c97d146cd9d5d9be17d963.svg)\nTherefore, the matrix which represents the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n    ![](images/5c/5cb3e4da19858e479f72aa783381f97e846d820117c141681d5791b09490f8e8.svg)"] [17592186087604 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n![](images/e0/e067e3639e15c47b320a582baeec5c280f376d96de0af75d163c1d5ca3cd82f1.svg)"] [17592186087604 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the complementary solution by solvin