teristic equation has roots ![](images/fc/fc98374a630d6269fbeaa595a26657a2a2a93c4fa54325ef1fa95e6f648bc5d3.svg) so the complementary solution is\n![](images/fd/fd5408e519fbfc48ea58ba46384bf4f9ece51a68ac3488bf9ac03a7978d06cd4.svg)\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is ![](images/ee/ee4cb113b8bd85252077b002ac0ae9e170bc04975254ed47385ab98104c7c8e6.svg) and ![](images/c2/c228482a36b98d24cb5db669b2ea5dbc6ba571979e4da7a7c80a77311a9a7de8.svg) is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form\n![](images/c3/c37a270e8e9982b6606729b52e4a3b9e3aff50e809545aae065e502c66b1f3ac.svg)\nwhere ![](images/79/7995b2daef1650484d2fa6518cb775356ae12ecdf597eba9e2e04f4f35dee93c.svg) and ![](images/f2/f27acf6959fc15846a7239dae3b1f8398bc7a1129e9bdf8175acc9476f7cbca0.svg) are constants to be determined.\n\n**Caution:** The terms of the complementary solution are ![](images/7e/7e6f0ca90c220b0a5cdef6c9340b306c4046de68f8652b17b7eba46d9b108d52.svg) and ![](images/9d/9d721df1e9ef99b68b600a35c96c1018191bb61c2722ef055174d33cfabee6cf.svg) Although ![](images/c2/c228482a36b98d24cb5db669b2ea5dbc6ba571979e4da7a7c80a77311a9a7de8.svg) appears, ![](images/c2/c228482a36b98d24cb5db669b2ea5dbc6ba571979e4da7a7c80a77311a9a7de8.svg) is not itself a term because it is multiplied by ![](images/d3/d34ab740163cb05013fa9215e5477416d899d8019bce5ac7af648477625b014a.svg)\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ![](images/4d/4d58e4bb5a428c380f2260e2338284a14084a2ec5dc1a88a8c7ac110e8e39c93.svg) we get\n![](images/8b/8b95b69c723d32b6decc46879f28b91149711ddf36c951afcd4bca584d562819.svg)\n\nTo find the values of ![](images/79/7995b2daef1650484d2fa6518cb775356ae12ecdf597eba9e2e04f4f35dee93c.svg) and ![](images/e5/e5f9648e0ed288008f7cba215426334563f09457530f9479a3ebd4918dca76d2.svg) we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n![](images/9c/9cd2ec7928ef4dde85caf25d3fb2c9e0a3cee570e7e9b39bb263f8f9dd45a000.svg)\n\nThe corresponding terms on both sides should have the same coefficients. Equating the coefficients of like terms, we get the following system:\n![](images/6b/6b227d6734ad4d87d42fb445a79396a2f68ed896f5baad98e74f6347c45d7245.svg)\n\nSolving this system gives ![](images/a4/a4ee605afe3f68a010f06c3343aff9d7f967a3b3fd68c5ad5caae3695b745910.svg)\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n![](images/5a/5a44cc55479bd8dd9c33482fdeb61eb6d2a2720bfe0abcca95cd8b2a9ba58dba.svg)\nand the general solution is\n![](images/74/74724ed0976cafdb18816dc3146552dee7ebbe4746fb01cf30858805facdeee1.svg)"] [17592186087608 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n![](images/73/73f0065d4d4768a2c3432559702c2db28fefd050194a5bcdfe44ac3e0b736c82.png)\n\nThe line segment $\\overline{AB}$ is shown below. Find $\\overline{A'B'},$ the image of $\\overline{AB}$ under the action of $\\mathbf T.$\n\n![](images/2e/2e22400d15abc81150d6d18de591549e81a46bd16ab9e95f10f86bae2125b490.png)"] [17592186087608 :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $\\overline{AB}$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the endpoints of $\\overline{AB}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/fe/fe82a0c8616e43c9d8db0b2892a145fcabf4cfa17bd3d4e033a1349cd25921ee.png)\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective endpoints of the image are $A'(1,3)$ and $B'(1,-2),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/47/472f8e712f7d695d5ab3593dafbcc0309feb1a6ac80e9e29c4893dc651d20cf7.png)"] [17592186087631 :question/problem "Find the minimum value of the parabola ![](images/d3/d30006cd23eb5bc4fab41b442fa7559a396842e82c35c3a481533208827abe28.svg) sketched below.\n\n![](images/1a/1af23738cbe57195d1bf4dc223c37fab61cb89058388445a67eadeaab88a14bd.png)"] [17592186087631 :question/worked-solution "The minimum value of the parabola is the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinate of the vertex of the parabola. \n\nRemember that the vertex form of a parabola is\n\n![](images/c5/c53ff35f8ae40bcb44f4cf26ad7f0d1bed0dabc73298562d7041cc90a0b1de3c.svg)\n\nwhere ![](images/3a/3a6333dccc007be0992f95974a58ce3d652e5ecafcaaa865a24ff8b42bcfc37a.svg) are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola.\n\nThe given parabola\n\n![](images/21/211afc137cc4072f18eb008836b3f2d8c231aa5ae017476147b0b50014525f39.svg)\n\nis written in vertex form with ![](images/03/033a93fcdc4a0358a5de03cbdda9860a8ac6aa6a735f3681a6477c3a3e52ea55.svg) and ![](images/10/1027d08706beef7b1afa836932edaac3ebad0885bb8a2be54860dc768d431ff9.svg) Therefore, the vertex of the parabola is at ![](images/79/795920c332cb28b6b824a8b84b1226b6d1e5b5f890e16ecd6752ce6fd619e37e.svg) and its minimum value is ![](images/d4/d44d7e189fa83441142c19838f09553ff4b73fba09689c5a04d1a6eaf5fef088.svg)"] [17592186088870 :tutorial/content "Up to now, all the parabolas we've seen have been written in **standard form**.\n\n![](images/e3/e3fb2f9598362bc373e475faf19bb84623a73281da549bf85018f22b7f8a095d.svg)\n\nWe can also write the equation of a parabola in the so-called **vertex form,** given by\n\n![](images/4d/4dc70525f24857744b03c32f2f558b27691c823f17bab16a569d3bbdadab732b.svg)\n\nWhen a parabola is written in vertex form, the pair ![](images/3a/3a6333dccc007be0992f95974a58ce3d652e5ecafcaaa865a24ff8b42bcfc37a.svg) gives the coordinates of its vertex.\n\nFor example, consider the following parabola: \n\n![](images/23/2366bd4f3ea2bcb500a85b13c29a3b512d0529ffc82e507c81dae3df96176981.svg)\n\nThis parabola is written in vertex form with ![](images/b4/b4edfee225234d67bf8c5a1f8283fe3eb5edba258f6268e9ffe7e604def55858.svg) and ![](images/6f/6fa7648f1eeb4effdf9b60cf5199b0ac26e1c1d57c0f05d9e9bd895e5c277c21.svg) Therefore, the vertex of the parabola is at ![](images/2b/2bea2c53403b70e552b749cbbf6a3eba65ebdf56f2028ee7907a6700bbedafc4.svg)\n\n![](images/4e/4e050c9f52965d9682cae055c1264ac2db9a58b09557f6e15f0178e9375b3872.png)\n\nIn future lessons, we'll learn how to express any parabola in vertex form. For now, let's get some practice working with this form."] [17592186088876 :tutorial/content "Consider the circle with equation \n![](images/04/04942c0d9aa30a9c5730d1e16a580d9352f71ae539072e45a9c027f2c2a24e23.svg)\nas shown below. How can we calculate the points where the circle intercepts the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis?\n\n![](images/9f/9f288ed4c4bf402576e0af193128e3a72866b4333be46b7cd7294e0e703cc8d4.png)\n\n \n\nWhen the circle intercepts the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis, we must have ![](images/29/29da0a4605613d6a7fd9e7bb2f4fb45cbabe8a47109971682844cb222486a20c.svg)\n\nTherefore, to find the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercepts of the circle, we need to set ![](images/51/51a24269e3f466a9c7b886bcab70a365576b28534e323a55aec8b3ebe8d14571.svg) in the equation of the circle and solve for ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg) as follows:\n\n![](images/c0/c0d3f8274a97b11ec1b29e9ac6f1594f153a558c54d40f3b0fe0a11cfc92ed34.svg)\n\nSo, the circle intercepts the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis at ![](images/02/02009543b06c29977f90e4d9650dae5609d3dc21630daf575e791866a131b661.svg) and ![](images/80/809ca990d60556175140938d3a8f1c4a52048f8993899089f6f1dce4ea6b69aa.svg)"] [17592186088912 :tutorial/content "When an ellipse intercepts the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis, we must have ![](images/29/29da0a4605613d6a7fd9e7bb2f4fb45cbabe8a47109971682844cb222486a20c.svg) So, to find the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercepts of an ellipse, we substitute ![](images/51/51a24269e3f466a9c7b886bcab70a365576b28534e323a55aec8b3ebe8d14571.svg) into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for ![](images/33/3337bbda5af4a3203ef0f6135b36de4f06c1915c4a3ff1bd5358326627c1eecd.svg)\n\nFor example, let's find the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercepts of the ellipse given by the equation\n\n![](images/56/5683b5e36c95c36af104a43f9925d36494600eb5800641dd6fee797a0e823cd1.svg)\n\nSubstituting ![](images/51/51a24269e3f466a9c7b886bcab70a365576b28534e323a55aec8b3ebe8d14571.svg) in the equation of the ellipse and solving the resulting equation for ![](images/f4/f4e3bd65ea5e543add33f4a03c10e6b51bba71ff45e1eee33a9ce7e01d064ac7.svg) we get\n![](images/f2/f2d9dd16a35b6be9f8f531de23c6c260904760a2ab07fca95ac5bdd2bd8da448.svg)\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis at the points ![](images/42/42f562f132f08838fe095cdeb883c38dd65e0d77828f65f73d783980c52f34ec.svg) and ![](images/89/890e85845685b97022a3d948405b315a6dcb34195a3a3c7e057c18c22327276f.svg) We can verify this by plotting the graph:\n\n![](images/bf/bf692dc0a62f43c014cd0dd9a704a624e5216f1d53bf8458679cfeab052394e1.png)"] [17592186088930 :tutorial/content "A **dilation** of scale factor $k$ with the center at the origin is a linear transformation that multiplies the standard basis vectors by $k.$ It is represented by the matrix\n![](images/0b/0bad96759d0f794111219092aebafa8242d11ab8adf1bd4dbec31d28f745c766.svg)\n\nFor example, let ![](images/f6/f6c5df003fc9570c1a7ee6111f778f79f27bdffd61d717ee3a5ebf6e53c9718e.svg) be the matrix of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}.$ This transformation is a dilation of scale factor $2.$\n\nTo visualize the dilation $\\mathbf{T},$ consider the standard basis $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square $\\mathrm{R}$ spanned by the vectors, as shown below. \n\n![](images/6e/6e38185922e9cad6d3961e7c5e755eabf4c52eafd1cc81a1e81a383b4df5ae82.png)\n\nWe can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =Ti=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=2i, \\\\ {j}^{′} & =Tj=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=2j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ multiplies vectors $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j}$ by $2.$ The resulting vectors span a larger region, shown below.\n\n![](images/cc/cc6677c641a2a713e0324db90edfdd86a5ca1c096c0ffd70f1216119f36da645.png)"] [17592186088931 :tutorial/content "A **reflection in the $x$-axis** is represented by the matrix\n![](images/96/96d1e42ccb0e19c6a7fe605af0cbd17ded39967798c9804bf7514adc845c44b8.svg)\n\nTo visualize a reflection in the $x$-axis, consider the standard basis $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square $\\mathrm{R}$ spanned by the vectors, as shown below. \n\n![](images/9e/9e322e6ed1ddb8f1d094633e931cd179b7eb9d6f47acd7c7d0809f113df91b22.png)\n\nWe can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =T(i)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =T(j)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we obtain the following image:\n\n![](images/b9/b93bb91dae85a3e3b40a8d0ade3d4f420064aaa6926431fa7f664c977bb8fd59.png)\n\nSimilarly, a **reflection in the $y$-axis** is represented by the matrix\n![](images/40/40e77688a74462b8d0d29bca87db283c4ccb960fad6f1ac7cbac716a73f7b8e0.svg)\n\nAgain, we can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =T(i)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=-i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =T(j)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtain the following image:\n\n![](images/49/4993d8586a84e4a40a54edddd8011cd3d516264bfffc7e3c33d27a10edf1e7d7.png)"] [17592186088932 :tutorial/content "Similar to transformations in a vector space, we can define a transformation between two *different* vector spaces. For instance, consider the transformation that maps the vector \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{S}: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} \\longrightarrow \\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}1}x+{\\color{blue}1}y \\\\ {\\color{blue}1}x+({\\color{blue}-1})y \\\\ {\\color{blue}2}x+{\\color{blue}2}y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nfrom $\\mathbb{R}^2$ to the vector \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x+y \\\\ x-y \\\\ 2x+2y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nin $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ which can be written as\n\n![](images/ac/ac48dfb4fa4df769cc32789336d9f76960bde087351adf44328c33ebb1c40545.svg)\n\nThe *standard matrix* of this transformation is \n\n![](images/0a/0a94602cefa1750e0833e75a9a1e368d5d4164703fc85319db56cbfc28512194.svg)\n\nRecall that a transformation $\\mathbf{T}: V \\to W$ between two vector spaces $V$ and $W$ is *linear* if for any vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2\\in V$ and any scalar $k\\in\\mathbb{R}$, the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- $\\mathbf{T}(k\\mathbf{v}_1) = k\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_1)$\n\n- $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_1+\\mathbf{v}_2) = \\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_1)+\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_2)$\n\nIt is easy to check that if a transformation can be represented as a matrix, then it is a linear transformation. Therefore, the transformation $\\mathbf{S}$ is a linear transformation.\n\nIn general, if $V$ and $W$ are $n$-dimensional and $m$-dimensional vector spaces respectively, then the standard matrix of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}\\!: V \\!\\rightarrow W$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix $T$."] [17592186088956 :tutorial/content "A **reflection in line $y=x$** is represented by the matrix\n![](images/d6/d6c5ff7f9c96510f6243059a023a32026a3ea0dd181a2b07bdf82147de887361.svg)\n\nWe can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $T$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =T(i)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=j \\\\ {j}^{′} & =T(j)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we obtain the following image:\n\n![](images/0e/0e37a258ea833a2d490fc34a34af40a5c7a18f7589f107c6d0ed631b331c8f2a.png)\n\nSimilarly, a **reflection in line $y=-x$** is represented by the matrix\n![](images/f4/f450c3581b23abb715ba02d7459b043f6414cbd06e50bafdafbdb011e4160625.svg)\n\nWe can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =T(i)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=-j \\\\ {j}^{′} & =T(j)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we obtain the following image:\n\n![](images/4a/4a83673a409fd2e5d33b0b5f743f57af08cfb27c38e312a636179102c2172b04.png)"] [17592186088990 :tutorial/content "Let's consider our first example once more.\n\n![](images/bc/bc15538fc507a42e4ef0cde0798d90cafca6d3d02e5e7c1630c6fc3e4a1e306b.png)\n\nYou might be wondering how we can tell that the vertex coordinates are ![](images/e2/e2002597cf3d8b01e24e7b4d2c6daf6fc9afb0afb3c6140877abfc3bf1be02c4.svg) just from looking at the equation. \n\nTo see this, let's label the two terms in our parabola formula:\n\n![](images/03/03e67590d6a4c3f9257b2330fcce119e3710397453b36fb0f9486964259ba40b.svg)\n\nNow, note the following:\n\n- The quadratic part ![](images/36/3658549ec19c3c38654ef48e3982b82008b2630ebd0de7f5fe04bd7d4d7073ef.svg) is a *squared* quantity. Therefore, it can only be *positive* or *zero*.\n\n- Moreover, the quadratic part *equals* zero when ![](images/d3/d3f2aa545ab3eb25dc10571bd58aefec57b90fbf67e3281039c937b753b48a6a.svg)\n\n- Since the constant part ![](images/07/07e82ee914d3f32c77556663cbc38417dc5f057c180e58a1fb4230bb364f5c25.svg) does not vary with ![](images/5a/5aac2c81021f3f9e468e7840c5f95ece35c10b894bdae19ec1d247bdeb47ac6f.svg) the entire expression is *minimized* when ![](images/d3/d3f2aa545ab3eb25dc10571bd58aefec57b90fbf67e3281039c937b753b48a6a.svg) Therefore, the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-coordinate of the minimum point (i.e., the vertex) is ![](images/27/270d33581e0657a10d567c6cfaa529521b34e20a39c9a29550698cbf252fabfa.svg)\n\n- When ![](images/48/480183a1321e5b4fdff9500a551818f6fb0ab2713c0fdd8fa9df88a640394af9.svg) the quadratic part is zero, giving ![](images/21/2182a4d19eb1c758d967edc4951247e3d512519ac6d8b95676bc51eb2fe57d1c.svg) Therefore, the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinate of the vertex is ![](images/da/daae2fd3251c4ba175906af980d1c33d4db057c616509ec6a146307d64417228.svg)\n\nFollowing this logic, we conclude that the coordinates of the vertex are ![](images/2b/2bea2c53403b70e552b749cbbf6a3eba65ebdf56f2028ee7907a6700bbedafc4.svg)\n\nFinally, it's possible to generalize this argument to any parabola. For ![](images/28/28e6012b2b10c0a7f40681ab8a73666a12afd6e976c59f536ed30d1defca5f64.svg) the coordinates ![](images/3a/3a6333dccc007be0992f95974a58ce3d652e5ecafcaaa865a24ff8b42bcfc37a.svg) give the maximum of the parabola."] [17592186091934 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T$ is a bivariate normal vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =(\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}),\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} \\\\ \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\sigma^2_i = \\textrm{Var}[X_i],$ $\\sigma_{12} = \\textrm{Cov}[X_1,X_2],$ and $\\rho$ is the correlation, then the joint probability density function of $\\mathbf X$ is\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf x) = \\dfrac{1}{2\\pi\\sqrt{|\\Sigma|}} \\mathrm{exp}\\left( -\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf x - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,\\Sigma^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf x - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right)\n$$\n\nand $|\\Sigma|$ denotes the determinant of $\\Sigma.$\n\nUsing the given values of $\\sigma^2_1, \\sigma^2_2$, and $\\rho,$ our covariance matrix is\n\n![](images/f4/f44326b7be98378285978658be508da822c110d5d61e8950872cc75c4296d7ae.svg)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|\\Sigma| = 9\\cdot 1 - 1\\cdot 1 = 8.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into our expression for the joint PDF, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{8}}exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\pi \\sqrt{2}}exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $k=4\\pi\\sqrt 2.$"] [17592186091936 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $\\mathbf{X} =  [X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_k]^T$ is a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma,$ then the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY= \\mathbf c^T \\mathbf X = \\sum_{i=1}^k c_i X_i\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable, where $\\mathbf c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu , \\\\ Var[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, since we wish to know the distribution of $Y = X_1 -2X_2 +4 X_3,$ we have \n\n![](images/12/129ff41cb0fc5917efdc4f990d3d2efeb7546911eadb52340156f1ddc4f9d92f.svg)\n\nTherefore, for the mean of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu  \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1-4+4 \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the variance of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =12-6+56 \\\\  & =62.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY\\sim N(1,62).\n$$"] [17592186091943 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$2^n > 2n$ for all $n \\geq 3, n\\in\\mathbb N$\n\nWhat are the missing entries in the proof template outlined below?\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case ![](images/55/55b1aa9ddb93f56c2237d8c47d8c4e74126c4f287690fa23f2142a4e926a77c6.svg)\n![](images/f4/f4a96cfa3b106bf988ce2c42346c1654863fe23d0e2db674e4273145c70d2bd2.svg)\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some ![](images/d1/d173450199d3b813b8260082f81b4030333e9e1b0e1949b8972c95a17428162b.svg) We have\n![](images/5b/5b2ed3cdec9017722c5123b54514d3f08787950c90d5b9e8993f45bd0f5fcbe2.svg)\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n![](images/85/858c9bbe6b754ef2f70be8a99babad3df452c10cb824f30fded9aa9b8b05189f.svg)\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, $2^n > 2n$ for all $n \\geq 3.$"] [17592186091944 :question/worked-solution "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A \\subseteq X$ is the function\n![](images/15/159dcc0eb98804f6d4d281603de45cf00922023ce1d427ff73a7188bea38e40d.svg)\n\nIn the diagram above, only the elements $1,2,$ and $5$ are mapped to $1.$ Therefore, the indicator function in the diagram above corresponds to the set $A=\\{ 1,2,5 \\}.$"] [17592186091979 :question/problem "Find the solutions to the equation ![](images/e7/e78d5dad8572fafb8d5c3bfcd21f327ae03eef8c905832d66dbe574950a80ea7.png) for ![](images/41/4179f0bf2f433d4bbf1c9b383bc915b7a76e0707116d324465573292f3445b52.png)"] [17592186091979 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating ![](images/7a/7af9ab4320e858ed7e250d7a02bd8da85fe0098e007bc4e85aa5f8e0d57356dd.png)\n\n![](images/b0/b0a47289f02aab007c547b802baf12e15bcd9f46cf020afa1bee9f7f6beda57b.png)\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n![](images/7d/7df61d21b0c92ff7bd69dc74cd261ab5121981595e2be36fcf8dcc9b3b8a8959.png)\n\nNote that ![](images/c2/c2f8d8f4c5c2617f120c090e4a3af4bdaff03a449ab37f9030d456db963764cb.png) is not in the domain ![](images/11/114fd23a8df71f5492fb05044bc09c1e8eae3d171fc652152e4f0eb75b1bf0e5.png) To get the first solution that's within the required domain, we need to find an angle that's coterminal with ![](images/8a/8af09f7610b6744fe62e815419cb28d2945dd0a0f9faf3e7d8c19e10a2695b09.png) To do this, we simply add ![](images/6b/6b1dd15ea6bd662acd97c6091027f69bbcdec06704e4b4dd32cdae874c37c7bf.png)\n\n![](images/ca/caa280985722063a885bfdfb7fd618690c50b068f93879d5bb16c94babd9b4f6.png) \n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ![](images/35/3